n evasion", "saas", "stealth", "t1696"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1696 **Tactics:** stealth **Platforms:** SaaS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1696 Adversaries may employ various means to evade or complete automation checks. Automation checks distinguish human users from bot traffic and prevent bots from reaching the web pages "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1697", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1697 Steal Application Specific Passwords", "tags": ["credential-access", "identity provider", "office suite", "saas", "steal application specific passwords", "t1697"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1697 **Tactics:** credential-access **Platforms:** Office Suite, Identity Provider, SaaS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1697 Adversaries may steal Application Specific Passwords (ASPs) to gain access to [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078),"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1698", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1698 Indirect Prompt Injection", "tags": ["execution", "indirect prompt injection", "saas", "t1698", "windows"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1698 **Tactics:** execution **Platforms:** Windows, SaaS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1698 Adversaries may deliver malicious prompts to an LLM indirectly by embedding the prompts in content that the adversary expects may be processed by an LLM.(Citation: NIST i"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1699", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1699 Direct Prompt Injection", "tags": ["direct prompt injection", "execution", "saas", "t1699", "windows"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1699 **Tactics:** execution **Platforms:** Windows, SaaS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1699 Adversaries may input malicious prompts directly into an LLM’s user interface.(Citation: NIST Direct Prompt Injection) The prompts can be designed to bypass safeguards, m"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1608-007", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1608.007 AI Result Poisoning", "tags": ["ai result poisoning", "pre", "resource-development", "t1608.007"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1608.007 **Tactics:** resource-development **Platforms:** PRE **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1608/007 Adversaries may manipulate publicly accessible web content to influence generative AI systems or AI-powered search engines into retrieving, citing, or recommendi"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1696-001", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1696.001 Automate CAPTCHA Completion", "tags": ["automate captcha completion", "saas", "stealth", "t1696.001"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1696.001 **Tactics:** stealth **Platforms:** SaaS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1696/001 Adversaries may attempt to use bots to successfully solve CAPTCHA challenges. CAPTCHA challenges are typically used by defenders to detect and prevent bot and spam traffic f"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1696-002", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1696.002 Avoid CAPTCHA", "tags": ["avoid captcha", "saas", "stealth", "t1696.002"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1696.002 **Tactics:** stealth **Platforms:** SaaS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1696/002 Adversaries may avoid CAPTCHA challenges by mimicking human behavior. Automation tests, such as CAPTCHA, are typically used by defenders to detect and prevent bot and spam t"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1685-007", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1685.007 Impair Autonomous Defenses", "tags": ["defense-impairment", "impair autonomous defenses", "saas", "t1685.007", "windows"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1685.007 **Tactics:** defense-impairment **Platforms:** Windows, SaaS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1685/007 Adversaries may use methods like [Indirect Prompt Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1698) to disrupt, disable, or otherwise impair autonomo"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1700", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1700 AI-Enabled Exfiltration", "tags": ["ai-enabled exfiltration", "exfiltration", "saas", "t1700", "windows"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1700 **Tactics:** exfiltration **Platforms:** Windows, SaaS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1700 Adversaries may utilize [Direct Prompt Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1699), [Indirect Prompt Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1698) or"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-credential-access", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Credential Access", "tags": ["credential access", "credential-access", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to steal account names, passwords, or other secrets that enable access to resources. Credential access represents techniques that can be used by adversaries to obtain access to or control over passwords, tokens, cryptographic keys, or other values that could be used by an adv"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-defense-impairment", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Defense Impairment", "tags": ["defense impairment", "defense-impairment", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to break security mechanisms, pipelines, and tooling so defenders can’t see or trust what’s happening. Defense Impairment consists of techniques that degrade, disable, or undermine the effectiveness and trustworthiness of security controls and monitoring mechanisms. These tec"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-execution", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Execution", "tags": ["execution", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to run code or manipulate system functions, parameters, and data in an unauthorized way. Execution consists of techniques that result in adversary-controlled code running on a local or remote system, device, or other asset. This execution may also rely on unknowing end users "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-impact", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Impact", "tags": ["impact", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to manipulate, interrupt, or destroy your ICS systems, data, and their surrounding environment. Impact consists of techniques that adversaries use to disrupt, compromise, destroy, and manipulate the integrity and availability of control system operations, processes, devices, "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-persistence", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Persistence", "tags": ["persistence", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to maintain their foothold in your ICS environment. Persistence consists of techniques that adversaries use to maintain access to ICS systems and devices across restarts, changed credentials, and other interruptions that could cut off their access. Techniques used for persist"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-privilege-escalation", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Privilege Escalation", "tags": ["privilege escalation", "privilege-escalation", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to gain higher-level permissions. Privilege Escalation consists of techniques that adversaries use to gain higher-level permissions on a system or network. Adversaries can often enter and explore a network with unprivileged access but require elevated permissions to follow th"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-lateral-movement", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Lateral Movement", "tags": ["lateral movement", "lateral-movement", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to move through your ICS environment. Lateral Movement consists of techniques that adversaries use to enter and control remote systems on a network. These techniques abuse default credentials, known accounts, and vulnerable services, and may also leverage dual-homed devices a"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-stealth", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Stealth", "tags": ["stealth", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to hide and conceal their actions, appearing as normal behavior. Stealth consists of techniques that reduce the likelihood of detection by blending in with legitimate activity or minimizing observable signals. These techniques are characterized by concealment behaviors, such "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-exfiltration", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Exfiltration", "tags": ["exfiltration", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to steal data. Exfiltration refers to techniques and attributes that result or aid in the adversary removing files and information from the targeted mobile device. In the mobile environment, mobile devices are frequently connected to networks outside enterprise control such a"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-discovery", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Discovery", "tags": ["discovery", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is locating information to assess and identify their targets in your environment. Discovery consists of techniques that adversaries use to survey your ICS environment and gain knowledge about the internal network, control system devices, and how their processes interact. These techniqu"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-collection", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Collection", "tags": ["collection", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to gather data of interest and domain knowledge on your ICS environment to inform their goal. Collection consists of techniques adversaries use to gather domain knowledge and obtain contextual feedback in an ICS environment. This tactic is often performed as part of [Discover"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-resource-development", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Resource Development", "tags": ["resource development", "resource-development", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to establish resources they can use to support operations. Resource Development consists of techniques that involve adversaries creating, purchasing, or compromising/stealing resources that can be used to support targeting. Such resources include infrastructure, accounts, or "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-reconnaissance", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Reconnaissance", "tags": ["reconnaissance", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to gather information they can use to plan future operations. Reconnaissance consists of techniques that involve adversaries actively or passively gathering information that can be used to support targeting. Such information may include details of the victim organization, inf"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-command-and-control", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Command and Control", "tags": ["command and control", "command-and-control", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to communicate with and control compromised systems, controllers, and platforms with access to your ICS environment. Command and Control consists of techniques that adversaries use to communicate with and send commands to compromised systems, devices, controllers, and platfor"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-initial-access", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Initial Access", "tags": ["initial access", "initial-access", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to get into your ICS environment. Initial Access consists of techniques that adversaries may use as entry vectors to gain an initial foothold within an ICS environment. These techniques include compromising operational technology assets, IT resources in the OT network, and ex"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1174", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1174 Password Filter DLL Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "password filter dll mitigation", "t1174"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1174 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1174 Ensure only valid password filters are registered. Filter DLLs must be present in Windows installation directory (<code>C:\\Windows\\System32\\</code> by default) of a domain controller and/or local computer with a correspon"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1151", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1151 Space after Filename Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "space after filename mitigation", "t1151"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1151 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1151 Prevent files from having a trailing space after the extension."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1148", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1148 HISTCONTROL Mitigation", "tags": ["histcontrol mitigation", "mitigation", "t1148"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1148 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1148 Prevent users from changing the <code>HISTCONTROL</code> environment variable (Citation: Securing bash history). Also, make sure that the <code>HISTCONTROL</code> environment variable is set to “ignoredup” instead of “ign"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1081", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1081 Credentials in Files Mitigation", "tags": ["credentials in files mitigation", "mitigation", "t1081"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1081 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1081 Establish an organizational policy that prohibits password storage in files. Ensure that developers and system administrators are aware of the risk associated with having plaintext passwords in software configuration file"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1162", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1162 Login Item Mitigation", "tags": ["login item mitigation", "mitigation", "t1162"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1162 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1162 Restrict users from being able to create their own login items. Additionally, holding the shift key during login prevents apps from opening automatically (Citation: Re-Open windows on Mac)."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1166", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1166 Setuid and Setgid Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "setuid and setgid mitigation", "t1166"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1166 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1166 Applications with known vulnerabilities or known shell escapes should not have the setuid or setgid bits set to reduce potential damage if an application is compromised. Additionally, the number of programs with setuid or"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1223", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1223 Compiled HTML File Mitigation", "tags": ["compiled html file mitigation", "mitigation", "t1223"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1223 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1223 Consider blocking download/transfer and execution of potentially uncommon file types known to be used in adversary campaigns, such as CHM files. (Citation: PaloAlto Preventing Opportunistic Attacks Apr 2016) Also consider"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1488", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1488 Data Destruction Mitigation", "tags": ["data destruction mitigation", "mitigation", "t1488"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1488 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1488 Consider implementing IT disaster recovery plans that contain procedures for taking regular data backups that can be used to restore organizational data.(Citation: Ready.gov IT DRP) Ensure backups are stored off system an"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1084", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1084 Windows Management Instrumentation Event Subscription Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1084", "windows management instrumentation event subscription mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1084 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1084 Disabling WMI services may cause system instability and should be evaluated to assess the impact to a network. By default, only administrators are allowed to connect remotely using WMI; restrict other users that are allow"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1044", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1044 File System Permissions Weakness Mitigation", "tags": ["file system permissions weakness mitigation", "mitigation", "t1044"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1044 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1044 Use auditing tools capable of detecting file system permissions abuse opportunities on systems within an enterprise and correct them. Limit privileges of user accounts and groups so that only authorized administrators can"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1103", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1103 AppInit DLLs Mitigation", "tags": ["appinit dlls mitigation", "mitigation", "t1103"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1103 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1103 Upgrade to Windows 8 or later and enable secure boot. Identify and block potentially malicious software that may be executed through AppInit DLLs by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools, like AppLocker, (Cita"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1159", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1159 Launch Agent Mitigation", "tags": ["launch agent mitigation", "mitigation", "t1159"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1159 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1159 Restrict user's abilities to create Launch Agents with group policy."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1031", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1031 Network Intrusion Prevention", "tags": ["m1031", "mitigation", "network intrusion prevention"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1031 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1031 Use intrusion detection signatures to block traffic at network boundaries."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1117", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1117 Regsvr32 Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "regsvr32 mitigation", "t1117"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1117 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1117 Microsoft's Enhanced Mitigation Experience Toolkit (EMET) Attack Surface Reduction (ASR) feature can be used to block regsvr32.exe from being used to bypass whitelisting. (Citation: Secure Host Baseline EMET)"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1147", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1147 Hidden Users Mitigation", "tags": ["hidden users mitigation", "mitigation", "t1147"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1147 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1147 If the computer is domain joined, then group policy can help restrict the ability to create or hide users. Similarly, preventing the modification of the <code>/Library/Preferences/com.apple.loginwindow</code> <code>Hide50"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1016", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1016 Vulnerability Scanning", "tags": ["m1016", "mitigation", "vulnerability scanning"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1016 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1016 Vulnerability scanning involves the automated or manual assessment of systems, applications, and networks to identify misconfigurations, unpatched software, or other security weaknesses. The process helps prioritize remed"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1009", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1009 Binary Padding Mitigation", "tags": ["binary padding mitigation", "mitigation", "t1009"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1009 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1009 Identify potentially malicious software that may be executed from a padded or otherwise obfuscated binary, and audit and/or block it by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools, like AppLocker, (Citation: Windows"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1144", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1144 Gatekeeper Bypass Mitigation", "tags": ["gatekeeper bypass mitigation", "mitigation", "t1144"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1144 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1144 Other tools should be used to supplement Gatekeeper's functionality. Additionally, system settings can prevent applications from running that haven't been downloaded through the Apple Store which can help mitigate some of"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1013", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1013 Port Monitors Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "port monitors mitigation", "t1013"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1013 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1013 Identify and block potentially malicious software that may persist in this manner by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools capable of monitoring DLL loads by processes running under SYSTEM permissions."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1035", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1035 Limit Access to Resource Over Network", "tags": ["limit access to resource over network", "m1035", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1035 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1035 Restrict access to network resources, such as file shares, remote systems, and services, to only those users, accounts, or systems with a legitimate business requirement. This can include employing technologies like netwo"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1155", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1155 AppleScript Mitigation", "tags": ["applescript mitigation", "mitigation", "t1155"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1155 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1155 Require that all AppleScript be signed by a trusted developer ID before being executed - this will prevent random AppleScript code from executing (Citation: applescript signing). This subjects AppleScript code to the same"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1029", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1029 Remote Data Storage", "tags": ["m1029", "mitigation", "remote data storage"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1029 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1029 Remote Data Storage focuses on moving critical data, such as security logs and sensitive files, to secure, off-host locations to minimize unauthorized access, tampering, or destruction by adversaries. By leveraging remote"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1037", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1037 Filter Network Traffic", "tags": ["filter network traffic", "m1037", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1037 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1037 Employ network appliances and endpoint software to filter ingress, egress, and lateral network traffic. This includes protocol-based filtering, enforcing firewall rules, and blocking or restricting traffic based on predef"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1021", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1021 Restrict Web-Based Content", "tags": ["m1021", "mitigation", "restrict web-based content"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1021 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1021 Restricting web-based content involves enforcing policies and technologies that limit access to potentially malicious websites, unsafe downloads, and unauthorized browser behaviors. This can include URL filtering, downloa"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1130", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1130 Install Root Certificate Mitigation", "tags": ["install root certificate mitigation", "mitigation", "t1130"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1130 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1130 HTTP Public Key Pinning (HPKP) is one method to mitigate potential man-in-the-middle situations where and adversary uses a mis-issued or fraudulent certificate to intercept encrypted communications by enforcing use of an "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1033", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1033 Limit Software Installation", "tags": ["limit software installation", "m1033", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1033 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1033 Prevent users or groups from installing unauthorized or unapproved software to reduce the risk of introducing malicious or vulnerable applications. This can be achieved through allowlists, software restriction policies, e"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1169", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1169 Sudo Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "sudo mitigation", "t1169"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1169 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1169 The sudoers file should be strictly edited such that passwords are always required and that users can’t spawn risky processes as users with higher privilege. By requiring a password, even if an adversary can get terminal "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1079", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1079 Multilayer Encryption Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "multilayer encryption mitigation", "t1079"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1079 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1079 Network intrusion detection and prevention systems that use network signatures to identify traffic for specific adversary malware can be used to mitigate activity at the network level. Use of encryption protocols may make"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1493", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1493 Transmitted Data Manipulation Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1493", "transmitted data manipulation mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1493 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1493 Identify critical business and system processes that may be targeted by adversaries and work to secure communications related to those processes against tampering. Encrypt all important data flows to reduce the impact of "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1013", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1013 Application Developer Guidance", "tags": ["application developer guidance", "m1013", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1013 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1013 Application Developer Guidance focuses on providing developers with the knowledge, tools, and best practices needed to write secure code, reduce vulnerabilities, and implement secure design principles. By integrating secu"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1019", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1019 System Firmware Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "system firmware mitigation", "t1019"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1019 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1019 Prevent adversary access to privileged accounts or access necessary to perform this technique. Check the integrity of the existing BIOS or EFI to determine if it is vulnerable to modification. Patch the BIOS and EFI as ne"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1002", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1002 Data Compressed Mitigation", "tags": ["data compressed mitigation", "mitigation", "t1002"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1002 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1002 Identify unnecessary system utilities, third-party tools, or potentially malicious software that may be used to compress files, and audit and/or block them by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools, like AppLoc"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1034", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1034 Limit Hardware Installation", "tags": ["limit hardware installation", "m1034", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1034 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1034 Prevent unauthorized users or groups from installing or using hardware, such as external drives, peripheral devices, or unapproved internal hardware components, by enforcing hardware usage policies and technical controls."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1017", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1017 User Training", "tags": ["m1017", "mitigation", "user training"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1017 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1017 User Training involves educating employees and contractors on recognizing, reporting, and preventing cyber threats that rely on human interaction, such as phishing, social engineering, and other manipulative techniques. C"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1022", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1022 Data Encrypted Mitigation", "tags": ["data encrypted mitigation", "mitigation", "t1022"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1022 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1022 Identify unnecessary system utilities, third-party tools, or potentially malicious software that may be used to encrypt files, and audit and/or block them by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools, like AppLock"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1052", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1052 User Account Control", "tags": ["m1052", "mitigation", "user account control"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1052 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1052 User Account Control (UAC) is a security feature in Microsoft Windows that prevents unauthorized changes to the operating system. UAC prompts users to confirm or provide administrator credentials when an action requires e"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1150", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1150 Plist Modification Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "plist modification mitigation", "t1150"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1150 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1150 Prevent plist files from being modified by users by making them read-only."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1028", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1028 Operating System Configuration", "tags": ["m1028", "mitigation", "operating system configuration"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1028 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1028 Operating System Configuration involves adjusting system settings and hardening the default configurations of an operating system (OS) to mitigate adversary exploitation and prevent abuse of system functionality. Proper O"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1077", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1077 Windows Admin Shares Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1077", "windows admin shares mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1077 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1077 Do not reuse local administrator account passwords across systems. Ensure password complexity and uniqueness such that the passwords cannot be cracked or guessed. Deny remote use of local admin credentials to log into sys"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1004", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1004 Winlogon Helper DLL Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1004", "winlogon helper dll mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1004 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1004 Limit the privileges of user accounts so that only authorized administrators can perform Winlogon helper changes. Identify and block potentially malicious software that may be executed through the Winlogon helper process "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1494", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1494 Runtime Data Manipulation Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "runtime data manipulation mitigation", "t1494"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1494 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1494 Identify critical business and system processes that may be targeted by adversaries and work to secure those systems against tampering. Prevent critical business and system processes from being replaced, overwritten, or r"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1183", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1183 Image File Execution Options Injection Mitigation", "tags": ["image file execution options injection mitigation", "mitigation", "t1183"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1183 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1183 This type of attack technique cannot be easily mitigated with preventive controls since it is based on the abuse of operating system design features. For example, mitigating all IFEO will likely have unintended side effec"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1186", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1186 Process Doppelgänging Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "process doppelgänging mitigation", "t1186"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1186 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1186 This type of attack technique cannot be easily mitigated with preventive controls or patched since it is based on the abuse of operating system design features. For example, mitigating specific API calls will likely have "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1107", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1107 File Deletion Mitigation", "tags": ["file deletion mitigation", "mitigation", "t1107"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1107 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1107 Identify unnecessary system utilities, third-party tools, or potentially malicious software that may be used to delete files, and audit and/or block them by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools like AppLocker"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1089", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1089 Disabling Security Tools Mitigation", "tags": ["disabling security tools mitigation", "mitigation", "t1089"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1089 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1089 Ensure proper process, registry, and file permissions are in place to prevent adversaries from disabling or interfering with security services."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1196", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1196 Control Panel Items Mitigation", "tags": ["control panel items mitigation", "mitigation", "t1196"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1196 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1196 This type of attack technique cannot be easily mitigated with preventive controls since it is based on the abuse of operating system design features. For example, mitigating specific Windows API calls and/or execution of "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1097", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1097 Pass the Ticket Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "pass the ticket mitigation", "t1097"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1097 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1097 Monitor domains for unusual credential logons. Limit credential overlap across systems to prevent the damage of credential compromise. Ensure that local administrator accounts have complex, unique passwords. Do not allow "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1483", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1483 Domain Generation Algorithms Mitigation", "tags": ["domain generation algorithms mitigation", "mitigation", "t1483"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1483 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1483 This technique may be difficult to mitigate since the domains can be registered just before they are used, and disposed shortly after. Malware researchers can reverse-engineer malware variants that use DGAs and determine "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1146", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1146 Clear Command History Mitigation", "tags": ["clear command history mitigation", "mitigation", "t1146"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1146 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1146 Preventing users from deleting or writing to certain files can stop adversaries from maliciously altering their <code>~/.bash_history</code> files. Additionally, making these environment variables readonly can make sure t"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1028", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1028 Windows Remote Management Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1028", "windows remote management mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1028 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1028 Disable the WinRM service. If the service is necessary, lock down critical enclaves with separate WinRM infrastructure, accounts, and permissions. Follow WinRM best practices on configuration of authentication methods and"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1053", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1053 Data Backup", "tags": ["data backup", "m1053", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1053 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1053 Data Backup involves taking and securely storing backups of data from end-user systems and critical servers. It ensures that data remains available in the event of system compromise, ransomware attacks, or other disruptio"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1160", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1160 Launch Daemon Mitigation", "tags": ["launch daemon mitigation", "mitigation", "t1160"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1160 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1160 Limit privileges of user accounts and remediate Privilege Escalation vectors so only authorized administrators can create new Launch Daemons."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1184", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1184 SSH Hijacking Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "ssh hijacking mitigation", "t1184"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1184 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1184 Ensure SSH key pairs have strong passwords and refrain from using key-store technologies such as ssh-agent unless they are properly protected. Ensure that all private keys are stored securely in locations where only the l"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1215", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1215 Kernel Modules and Extensions Mitigation", "tags": ["kernel modules and extensions mitigation", "mitigation", "t1215"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1215 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1215 Common tools for detecting Linux rootkits include: rkhunter (Citation: SourceForge rkhunter), chrootkit (Citation: Chkrootkit Main), although rootkits may be designed to evade certain detection tools. LKMs and Kernel exte"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1214", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1214 Credentials in Registry Mitigation", "tags": ["credentials in registry mitigation", "mitigation", "t1214"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1214 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1214 Do not store credentials within the Registry. Proactively search for credentials within Registry keys and attempt to remediate the risk. If necessary software must store credentials, then ensure those accounts have limite"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1038", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1038 Execution Prevention", "tags": ["execution prevention", "m1038", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1038 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1038 Prevent the execution of unauthorized or malicious code on systems by implementing application control, script blocking, and other execution prevention mechanisms. This ensures that only trusted and authorized code is exe"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1043", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1043 Credential Access Protection", "tags": ["credential access protection", "m1043", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1043 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1043 Credential Access Protection focuses on implementing measures to prevent adversaries from obtaining credentials, such as passwords, hashes, tokens, or keys, that could be used for unauthorized access. This involves restri"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1066", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1066 Indicator Removal from Tools Mitigation", "tags": ["indicator removal from tools mitigation", "mitigation", "t1066"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1066 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1066 Mitigation is difficult in instances like this because the adversary may have access to the system through another channel and can learn what techniques or tools are blocked by resident defenses. Exercising best practices"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1156", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1156 .bash_profile and .bashrc Mitigation", "tags": [".bash_profile and .bashrc mitigation", "mitigation", "t1156"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1156 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1156 Making these files immutable and only changeable by certain administrators will limit the ability for adversaries to easily create user level persistence."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1076", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1076 Remote Desktop Protocol Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "remote desktop protocol mitigation", "t1076"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1076 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1076 Disable the RDP service if it is unnecessary, remove unnecessary accounts and groups from Remote Desktop Users groups, and enable firewall rules to block RDP traffic between network security zones. Audit the Remote Deskto"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1171", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1171 LLMNR/NBT-NS Poisoning Mitigation", "tags": ["llmnr/nbt-ns poisoning mitigation", "mitigation", "t1171"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1171 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1171 Disable LLMNR and NetBIOS in local computer security settings or by group policy if they are not needed within an environment. (Citation: ADSecurity Windows Secure Baseline) Use host-based security software to block LLMNR"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1142", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1142 Keychain Mitigation", "tags": ["keychain mitigation", "mitigation", "t1142"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1142 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1142 The password for the user's login keychain can be changed from the user's login password. This increases the complexity for an adversary because they need to know an additional password."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1045", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1045 Code Signing", "tags": ["code signing", "m1045", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1045 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1045 Code Signing is a security process that ensures the authenticity and integrity of software by digitally signing executables, scripts, and other code artifacts. It prevents untrusted or malicious code from executing by ver"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1099", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1099 Timestomp Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1099", "timestomp mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1099 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1099 Mitigation of timestomping specifically is likely difficult. Efforts should be focused on preventing potentially malicious software from running. Identify and block potentially malicious software that may contain function"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1039", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1039 Environment Variable Permissions", "tags": ["environment variable permissions", "m1039", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1039 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1039 Restrict the modification of environment variables to authorized users and processes by enforcing strict permissions and policies. This ensures the integrity of environment variables, preventing adversaries from abusing o"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1164", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1164 Re-opened Applications Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "re-opened applications mitigation", "t1164"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1164 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1164 Holding the Shift key while logging in prevents apps from opening automatically (Citation: Re-Open windows on Mac). This feature can be disabled entirely with the following terminal command: <code>defaults write -g AppleP"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1128", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1128 Netsh Helper DLL Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "netsh helper dll mitigation", "t1128"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1128 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1128 Identify and block potentially malicious software that may persist in this manner by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools capable of monitoring DLL loads by Windows utilities like AppLocker. (Citation: Window"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1172", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1172 Domain Fronting Mitigation", "tags": ["domain fronting mitigation", "mitigation", "t1172"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1172 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1172 If it is possible to inspect HTTPS traffic, the captures can be analyzed for connections that appear to be Domain Fronting. In order to use domain fronting, attackers will likely need to deploy additional tools to comprom"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1057", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1057 Data Loss Prevention", "tags": ["data loss prevention", "m1057", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1057 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1057 Data Loss Prevention (DLP) involves implementing strategies and technologies to identify, categorize, monitor, and control the movement of sensitive data within an organization. This includes protecting data formats indic"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1109", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1109 Component Firmware Mitigation", "tags": ["component firmware mitigation", "mitigation", "t1109"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1109 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1109 Prevent adversary access to privileged accounts or access necessary to perform this technique. Consider removing and replacing system components suspected of being compromised."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1025", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1025 Privileged Process Integrity", "tags": ["m1025", "mitigation", "privileged process integrity"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1025 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1025 Privileged Process Integrity focuses on defending highly privileged processes (e.g., system services, antivirus, or authentication processes) from tampering, injection, or compromise by adversaries. These processes often "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1188", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1188 Multi-hop Proxy Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "multi-hop proxy mitigation", "t1188"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1188 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1188 Traffic to known anonymity networks and C2 infrastructure can be blocked through the use of network black and white lists. It should be noted that this kind of blocking may be circumvented by other techniques like [Domain"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1161", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1161 LC_LOAD_DYLIB Addition Mitigation", "tags": ["lc_load_dylib addition mitigation", "mitigation", "t1161"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1161 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1161 Enforce that all binaries be signed by the correct Apple Developer IDs, and whitelist applications via known hashes. Binaries can also be baselined for what dynamic libraries they require, and if an app requires a new dyn"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1055", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1055 Do Not Mitigate", "tags": ["do not mitigate", "m1055", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1055 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1055 The Do Not Mitigate category highlights scenarios where attempting to mitigate a specific technique may inadvertently increase the organization's security risk or operational instability. This could happen due to the comp"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1056", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1056 Pre-compromise", "tags": ["m1056", "mitigation", "pre-compromise"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1056 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1056 Pre-compromise mitigations involve proactive measures and defenses implemented to prevent adversaries from successfully identifying and exploiting weaknesses during the Reconnaissance and Resource Development phases of an"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1073", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1073 DLL Side-Loading Mitigation", "tags": ["dll side-loading mitigation", "mitigation", "t1073"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1073 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1073 Update software regularly. Install software in write-protected locations. Use the program sxstrace.exe that is included with Windows along with manual inspection to check manifest files for side-loading vulnerabilities in"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1177", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1177 LSASS Driver Mitigation", "tags": ["lsass driver mitigation", "mitigation", "t1177"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1177 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1177 On Windows 8.1 and Server 2012 R2, enable LSA Protection by setting the Registry key <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\\RunAsPPL</code> to <code>dword:00000001</code>. (Citation: Microsoft LSA P"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1179", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1179 Hooking Mitigation", "tags": ["hooking mitigation", "mitigation", "t1179"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1179 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1179 This type of attack technique cannot be easily mitigated with preventive controls since it is based on the abuse of operating system design features. For example, mitigating all hooking will likely have unintended side ef"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1020", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1020 SSL/TLS Inspection", "tags": ["m1020", "mitigation", "ssl/tls inspection"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1020 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1020 SSL/TLS inspection involves decrypting encrypted network traffic to examine its content for signs of malicious activity. This capability is crucial for detecting threats that use encryption to evade detection, such as phi"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1093", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1093 Process Hollowing Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "process hollowing mitigation", "t1093"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1093 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1093 This type of attack technique cannot be easily mitigated with preventive controls since it is based on the abuse of operating system design features. For example, mitigating specific API calls will likely have unintended "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1046", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1046 Boot Integrity", "tags": ["boot integrity", "m1046", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1046 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1046 Boot Integrity ensures that a system starts securely by verifying the integrity of its boot process, operating system, and associated components. This mitigation focuses on leveraging secure boot mechanisms, hardware-root"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1154", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1154 Trap Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1154", "trap mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1154 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1154 Due to potential legitimate uses of trap commands, it's may be difficult to mitigate use of this technique."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1060", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1060 Out-of-Band Communications Channel", "tags": ["m1060", "mitigation", "out-of-band communications channel"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1060 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1060 Establish secure out-of-band communication channels to ensure the continuity of critical communications during security incidents, data integrity attacks, or in-network communication failures. Out-of-band communication re"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1173", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1173 Dynamic Data Exchange Mitigation", "tags": ["dynamic data exchange mitigation", "mitigation", "t1173"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1173 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1173 Registry keys specific to Microsoft Office feature control security can be set to disable automatic DDE/OLE execution. (Citation: Microsoft DDE Advisory Nov 2017) (Citation: BleepingComputer DDE Disabled in Word Dec 2017)"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1116", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1116 Code Signing Mitigation", "tags": ["code signing mitigation", "mitigation", "t1116"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1116 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1116 Process whitelisting and trusted publishers to verify authenticity of software can help prevent signed malicious or untrusted code from executing on a system. (Citation: NSA MS AppLocker) (Citation: TechNet Trusted Publis"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1501", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1501 Systemd Service Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "systemd service mitigation", "t1501"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1501 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1501 The creation and modification of systemd service unit files is generally reserved for administrators such as the Linux root user and other users with superuser privileges. Limit user access to system utilities such as sys"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1158", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1158 Hidden Files and Directories Mitigation", "tags": ["hidden files and directories mitigation", "mitigation", "t1158"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1158 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1158 Mitigation of this technique may be difficult and unadvised due to the the legitimate use of hidden files and directories."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1030", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1030 Network Segmentation", "tags": ["m1030", "mitigation", "network segmentation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1030 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1030 Network segmentation involves dividing a network into smaller, isolated segments to control and limit the flow of traffic between devices, systems, and applications. By segmenting networks, organizations can reduce the at"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1019", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1019 Threat Intelligence Program", "tags": ["m1019", "mitigation", "threat intelligence program"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1019 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1019 A Threat Intelligence Program enables organizations to proactively identify, analyze, and act on cyber threats by leveraging internal and external data sources. The program supports decision-making processes, prioritizes "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1141", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1141 Input Prompt Mitigation", "tags": ["input prompt mitigation", "mitigation", "t1141"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1141 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1141 This technique exploits users' tendencies to always supply credentials when prompted, which makes it very difficult to mitigate. Use user training as a way to bring awareness and raise suspicion for potentially malicious "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1060", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1060 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "registry run keys / startup folder mitigation", "t1060"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1060 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1060 Identify and block potentially malicious software that may be executed through run key or startup folder persistence using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools like AppLocker (Citation: Windows Commands JPCERT) (Ci"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1085", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1085 Rundll32 Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "rundll32 mitigation", "t1085"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1085 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1085 Microsoft's Enhanced Mitigation Experience Toolkit (EMET) Attack Surface Reduction (ASR) feature can be used to block methods of using rundll32.exe to bypass whitelisting. (Citation: Secure Host Baseline EMET)"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1193", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1193 Spearphishing Attachment Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "spearphishing attachment mitigation", "t1193"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1193 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1193 Network intrusion prevention systems and systems designed to scan and remove malicious email attachments can be used to block activity. Solutions can be signature and behavior based, but adversaries may construct attachme"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1027", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1027 Password Policies", "tags": ["m1027", "mitigation", "password policies"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1027 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1027 Set and enforce secure password policies for accounts to reduce the likelihood of unauthorized access. Strong password policies include enforcing password complexity, requiring regular password changes, and preventing pas"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1040", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1040 Behavior Prevention on Endpoint", "tags": ["behavior prevention on endpoint", "m1040", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1040 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1040 Behavior Prevention on Endpoint refers to the use of technologies and strategies to detect and block potentially malicious activities by analyzing the behavior of processes, files, API calls, and other endpoint events. Ra"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1191", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1191 CMSTP Mitigation", "tags": ["cmstp mitigation", "mitigation", "t1191"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1191 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1191 CMSTP.exe may not be necessary within a given environment (unless using it for VPN connection installation). Consider using application whitelisting configured to block execution of CMSTP.exe if it is not required for a g"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1058", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1058 Service Registry Permissions Weakness Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "service registry permissions weakness mitigation", "t1058"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1058 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1058 Ensure proper permissions are set for Registry hives to prevent users from modifying keys for system components that may lead to privilege escalation. Identify and block potentially malicious software that may be executed"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1018", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1018 User Account Management", "tags": ["m1018", "mitigation", "user account management"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1018 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1018 User Account Management involves implementing and enforcing policies for the lifecycle of user accounts, including creation, modification, and deactivation. Proper account management reduces the attack surface by limiting"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1131", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1131 Authentication Package Mitigation", "tags": ["authentication package mitigation", "mitigation", "t1131"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1131 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1131 Windows 8.1, Windows Server 2012 R2, and later versions, may make LSA run as a Protected Process Light (PPL) by setting the Registry key <code>HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\\RunAsPPL</code>, which requires all "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1165", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1165 Startup Items Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "startup items mitigation", "t1165"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1165 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1165 Since StartupItems are deprecated, preventing all users from writing to the <code>/Library/StartupItems</code> directory would prevent any startup items from getting registered. Similarly, appropriate permissions should b"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1126", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1126 Network Share Connection Removal Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "network share connection removal mitigation", "t1126"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1126 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1126 Follow best practices for mitigation of activity related to establishing [Windows Admin Shares](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1077). Identify unnecessary system utilities or potentially malicious software that may "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1182", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1182 AppCert DLLs Mitigation", "tags": ["appcert dlls mitigation", "mitigation", "t1182"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1182 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1182 Identify and block potentially malicious software that may be executed through AppCert DLLs by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools, like AppLocker, (Citation: Windows Commands JPCERT) (Citation: NSA MS AppLo"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1067", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1067 Bootkit Mitigation", "tags": ["bootkit mitigation", "mitigation", "t1067"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1067 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1067 Ensure proper permissions are in place to help prevent adversary access to privileged accounts necessary to perform this action. Use Trusted Platform Module technology and a secure or trusted boot process to prevent syste"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1038", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1038 DLL Search Order Hijacking Mitigation", "tags": ["dll search order hijacking mitigation", "mitigation", "t1038"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1038 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1038 Disallow loading of remote DLLs. (Citation: Microsoft DLL Preloading) This is included by default in Windows Server 2012+ and is available by patch for XP+ and Server 2003+. (Citation: Microsoft DLL Search) Path Algorithm"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1022", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1022 Restrict File and Directory Permissions", "tags": ["m1022", "mitigation", "restrict file and directory permissions"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1022 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1022 Restricting file and directory permissions involves setting access controls at the file system level to limit which users, groups, or processes can read, write, or execute files. By configuring permissions appropriately, "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1026", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1026 Privileged Account Management", "tags": ["m1026", "mitigation", "privileged account management"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1026 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1026 Privileged Account Management focuses on implementing policies, controls, and tools to securely manage privileged accounts (e.g., SYSTEM, root, or administrative accounts). This includes restricting access, limiting the s"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1180", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1180 Screensaver Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "screensaver mitigation", "t1180"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1180 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1180 Block .scr files from being executed from non-standard locations. Set Group Policy to force users to have a dedicated screensaver where local changes should not override the settings to prevent changes. Use Group Policy t"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1101", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1101 Security Support Provider Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "security support provider mitigation", "t1101"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1101 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1101 Windows 8.1, Windows Server 2012 R2, and later versions may make LSA run as a Protected Process Light (PPL) by setting the Registry key <code>HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\\RunAsPPL</code>, which requires all S"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1065", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1065 Uncommonly Used Port Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1065", "uncommonly used port mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1065 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1065 Properly configure firewalls and proxies to limit outgoing traffic to only necessary ports. Network intrusion detection and prevention systems that use network signatures to identify traffic for specific adversary malware"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1023", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1023 Shortcut Modification Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "shortcut modification mitigation", "t1023"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1023 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1023 Limit permissions for who can create symbolic links in Windows to appropriate groups such as Administrators and necessary groups for virtualization. This can be done through GPO: Computer Configuration > [Policies] > Wind"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1209", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1209 Time Providers Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1209", "time providers mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1209 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1209 Identify and block potentially malicious software that may be executed as a time provider by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools, like AppLocker, (Citation: Windows Commands JPCERT) (Citation: NSA MS AppLock"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1024", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1024 Restrict Registry Permissions", "tags": ["m1024", "mitigation", "restrict registry permissions"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1024 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1024 Restricting registry permissions involves configuring access control settings for sensitive registry keys and hives to ensure that only authorized users or processes can make modifications. By limiting access, organizatio"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1208", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1208 Kerberoasting Mitigation", "tags": ["kerberoasting mitigation", "mitigation", "t1208"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1208 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1208 Ensure strong password length (ideally 25+ characters) and complexity for service accounts and that these passwords periodically expire. (Citation: AdSecurity Cracking Kerberos Dec 2015) Also consider using Group Managed "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1024", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1024 Custom Cryptographic Protocol Mitigation", "tags": ["custom cryptographic protocol mitigation", "mitigation", "t1024"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1024 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1024 Network intrusion detection and prevention systems that use network signatures to identify traffic for specific adversary malware can be used to mitigate activity at the network level. Since the custom protocol used may n"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1049", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1049 Antivirus/Antimalware", "tags": ["antivirus/antimalware", "m1049", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1049 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1049 Antivirus/Antimalware solutions utilize signatures, heuristics, and behavioral analysis to detect, block, and remediate malicious software, including viruses, trojans, ransomware, and spyware. These solutions continuously"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1032", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1032 Standard Cryptographic Protocol Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "standard cryptographic protocol mitigation", "t1032"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1032 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1032 Network intrusion detection and prevention systems that use network signatures to identify traffic for specific adversary malware can be used to mitigate activity at the network level. Use of encryption protocols may make"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1121", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1121 Regsvcs/Regasm Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "regsvcs/regasm mitigation", "t1121"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1121 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1121 Regsvcs and Regasm may not be necessary within a given environment. Block execution of Regsvcs.exe and Regasm.exe if they are not required for a given system or network to prevent potential misuse by adversaries."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1096", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1096 NTFS File Attributes Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "ntfs file attributes mitigation", "t1096"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1096 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1096 It may be difficult or inadvisable to block access to EA and ADSs. (Citation: Microsoft ADS Mar 2014) (Citation: Symantec ADS May 2009) Efforts should be focused on preventing potentially malicious software from running. "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1139", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1139 Bash History Mitigation", "tags": ["bash history mitigation", "mitigation", "t1139"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1139 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1139 There are multiple methods of preventing a user's command history from being flushed to their .bash_history file, including use of the following commands: <code>set +o history</code> and <code>set -o history</code> to sta"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1192", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1192 Spearphishing Link Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "spearphishing link mitigation", "t1192"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1192 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1192 Because this technique involves user interaction on the endpoint, it's difficult to fully mitigate. However, there are potential mitigations. Users can be trained to identify social engineering techniques and spearphishin"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1500", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1500 Compile After Delivery Mitigation", "tags": ["compile after delivery mitigation", "mitigation", "t1500"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1500 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1500 This type of technique cannot be easily mitigated with preventive controls or patched since it is based on the abuse of operating system design features. For example, blocking all file compilation may have unintended side"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1032", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1032 Multi-factor Authentication", "tags": ["m1032", "mitigation", "multi-factor authentication"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1032 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1032 Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) enhances security by requiring users to provide at least two forms of verification to prove their identity before granting access. These factors typically include: - *Something you know*:"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1054", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1054 Software Configuration", "tags": ["m1054", "mitigation", "software configuration"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1054 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1054 Software configuration refers to making security-focused adjustments to the settings of applications, middleware, databases, or other software to mitigate potential threats. These changes help reduce the attack surface, e"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1050", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1050 New Service Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "new service mitigation", "t1050"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1050 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1050 Limit privileges of user accounts and remediate Privilege Escalation vectors so only authorized administrators can create new services. Identify and block unnecessary system utilities or potentially malicious software tha"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1178", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1178 SID-History Injection Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "sid-history injection mitigation", "t1178"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1178 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1178 Clean up SID-History attributes after legitimate account migration is complete. Consider applying SID Filtering to interforest trusts, such as forest trusts and external trusts, to exclude SID-History from requests to acc"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1048", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1048 Application Isolation and Sandboxing", "tags": ["application isolation and sandboxing", "m1048", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1048 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1048 Application Isolation and Sandboxing refers to the technique of restricting the execution of code to a controlled and isolated environment (e.g., a virtual environment, container, or sandbox). This method prevents potenti"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1100", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1100 Web Shell Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1100", "web shell mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1100 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1100 Ensure that externally facing Web servers are patched regularly to prevent adversary access through [Exploitation for Privilege Escalation](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1068) to gain remote code access or through "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1075", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1075 Pass the Hash Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "pass the hash mitigation", "t1075"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1075 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1075 Monitor systems and domain logs for unusual credential logon activity. Prevent access to [Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078). Apply patch KB2871997 to Windows 7 and higher systems to limit the defa"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1063", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1063 Security Software Discovery Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "security software discovery mitigation", "t1063"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1063 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1063 Identify unnecessary system utilities or potentially malicious software that may be used to acquire information about local security software, and audit and/or block them by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) too"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1088", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1088 Bypass User Account Control Mitigation", "tags": ["bypass user account control mitigation", "mitigation", "t1088"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1088 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1088 Remove users from the local administrator group on systems. Although UAC bypass techniques exist, it is still prudent to use the highest enforcement level for UAC when possible and mitigate bypass opportunities that exist"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1015", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1015 Accessibility Features Mitigation", "tags": ["accessibility features mitigation", "mitigation", "t1015"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1015 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1015 To use this technique remotely, an adversary must use it in conjunction with RDP. Ensure that Network Level Authentication is enabled to force the remote desktop session to authenticate before the session is created and t"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1163", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1163 Rc.common Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "rc.common mitigation", "t1163"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1163 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1163 Limit privileges of user accounts so only authorized users can edit the rc.common file."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1168", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1168 Local Job Scheduling Mitigation", "tags": ["local job scheduling mitigation", "mitigation", "t1168"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1168 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1168 Limit privileges of user accounts and remediate Privilege Escalation vectors so only authorized users can create scheduled jobs. Identify and block unnecessary system utilities or potentially malicious software that may b"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1194", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1194 Spearphishing via Service Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "spearphishing via service mitigation", "t1194"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1194 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1194 Determine if certain social media sites, personal webmail services, or other service that can be used for spearphishing is necessary for business operations and consider blocking access if activity cannot be monitored wel"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1017", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1017 Application Deployment Software Mitigation", "tags": ["application deployment software mitigation", "mitigation", "t1017"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1017 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1017 Grant access to application deployment systems only to a limited number of authorized administrators. Ensure proper system and access isolation for critical network systems through use of firewalls, account privilege sepa"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1045", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1045 Software Packing Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "software packing mitigation", "t1045"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1045 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1045 Ensure updated virus definitions. Create custom signatures for observed malware. Employ heuristic-based malware detection. Identify and prevent execution of potentially malicious software that may have been packed by usin"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1181", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1181 Extra Window Memory Injection Mitigation", "tags": ["extra window memory injection mitigation", "mitigation", "t1181"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1181 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1181 This type of attack technique cannot be easily mitigated with preventive controls since it is based on the abuse of operating system design features. For example, mitigating specific API calls will likely have unintended "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1047", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1047 Audit", "tags": ["audit", "m1047", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1047 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1047 Auditing is the process of recording activity and systematically reviewing and analyzing the activity and system configurations. The primary purpose of auditing is to detect anomalies and identify potential threats or wea"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1138", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1138 Application Shimming Mitigation", "tags": ["application shimming mitigation", "mitigation", "t1138"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1138 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1138 There currently aren't a lot of ways to mitigate application shimming. Disabling the Shim Engine isn't recommended because Windows depends on shimming for interoperability and software may become unstable or not work. Mic"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1086", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1086 PowerShell Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "powershell mitigation", "t1086"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1086 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1086 It may be possible to remove PowerShell from systems when not needed, but a review should be performed to assess the impact to an environment, since it could be in use for many legitimate purposes and administrative funct"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1050", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1050 Exploit Protection", "tags": ["exploit protection", "m1050", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1050 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1050 Deploy capabilities that detect, block, and mitigate conditions indicative of software exploits. These capabilities aim to prevent exploitation by addressing vulnerabilities, monitoring anomalous behaviors, and applying e"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1170", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1170 Mshta Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "mshta mitigation", "t1170"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1170 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1170 Mshta.exe may not be necessary within a given environment since its functionality is tied to older versions of Internet Explorer that have reached end of life. Use application whitelisting configured to block execution of"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1035", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1035 Service Execution Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "service execution mitigation", "t1035"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1035 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1035 Ensure that permissions disallow services that run at a higher permissions level from being created or interacted with by a user with a lower permission level. Also ensure that high permission level service binaries canno"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1042", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1042 Change Default File Association Mitigation", "tags": ["change default file association mitigation", "mitigation", "t1042"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1042 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1042 Direct mitigation of this technique is not recommended since it is a legitimate function that can be performed by users for software preferences. Follow Microsoft's best practices for file associations. (Citation: MSDN Fi"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1206", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1206 Sudo Caching Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "sudo caching mitigation", "t1206"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1206 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1206 Setting the <code>timestamp_timeout</code> to 0 will require the user to input their password every time <code>sudo</code> is executed. Similarly, ensuring that the <code>tty_tickets</code> setting is enabled will prevent"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1157", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1157 Dylib Hijacking Mitigation", "tags": ["dylib hijacking mitigation", "mitigation", "t1157"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1157 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1157 Prevent users from being able to write files to the search paths for applications, both in the folders where applications are run from and the standard dylib folders. If users can't write to these directories, then they c"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1152", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1152 Launchctl Mitigation", "tags": ["launchctl mitigation", "mitigation", "t1152"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1152 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1152 Prevent users from installing their own launch agents or launch daemons and instead require them to be pushed out by group policy."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1015", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1015 Active Directory Configuration", "tags": ["active directory configuration", "m1015", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1015 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1015 Implement robust Active Directory (AD) configurations using group policies to secure user accounts, control access, and minimize the attack surface. AD configurations enable centralized control over account settings, logo"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1051", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1051 Update Software", "tags": ["m1051", "mitigation", "update software"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1051 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1051 Software updates ensure systems are protected against known vulnerabilities by applying patches and upgrades provided by vendors. Regular updates reduce the attack surface and prevent adversaries from exploiting known sec"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1044", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1044 Restrict Library Loading", "tags": ["m1044", "mitigation", "restrict library loading"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1044 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1044 Restricting library loading involves implementing security controls to ensure that only trusted and verified libraries (DLLs, shared objects, etc.) are loaded into processes. Adversaries often abuse Dynamic-Link Library ("}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1492", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1492 Stored Data Manipulation Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "stored data manipulation mitigation", "t1492"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1492 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1492 Identify critical business and system processes that may be targeted by adversaries and work to secure the data related to those processes against tampering. Ensure least privilege principles are applied to important info"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1042", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1042 Disable or Remove Feature or Program", "tags": ["disable or remove feature or program", "m1042", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1042 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1042 Disable or remove unnecessary and potentially vulnerable software, features, or services to reduce the attack surface and prevent abuse by adversaries. This involves identifying software or features that are no longer nee"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1118", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1118 InstallUtil Mitigation", "tags": ["installutil mitigation", "mitigation", "t1118"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1118 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1118 InstallUtil may not be necessary within a given environment. Use application whitelisting configured to block execution of InstallUtil.exe if it is not required for a given system or network to prevent potential misuse by"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1054", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1054 Indicator Blocking Mitigation", "tags": ["indicator blocking mitigation", "mitigation", "t1054"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1054 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1054 Ensure event tracers/forwarders (Citation: Microsoft ETW May 2018), firewall policies, and other associated mechanisms are secured with appropriate permissions and access controls. Consider automatically relaunching forwa"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1198", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1198 SIP and Trust Provider Hijacking Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "sip and trust provider hijacking mitigation", "t1198"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1198 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1198 Ensure proper permissions are set for Registry hives to prevent users from modifying keys related to SIP and trust provider components. Also ensure that these values contain their full path to prevent [DLL Search Order Hi"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1145", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1145 Private Keys Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "private keys mitigation", "t1145"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1145 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1145 Use strong passphrases for private keys to make cracking difficult. When possible, store keys on separate cryptographic hardware instead of on the local system. Ensure only authorized keys are allowed access to critical r"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1094", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1094 Custom Command and Control Protocol Mitigation", "tags": ["custom command and control protocol mitigation", "mitigation", "t1094"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1094 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1094 Properly configure firewalls and proxies to limit outgoing traffic to only necessary ports and through proper network gateway systems. Also ensure hosts are only provisioned to communicate over authorized interfaces. Netw"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1036", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1036 Account Use Policies", "tags": ["account use policies", "m1036", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1036 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1036 Account Use Policies help mitigate unauthorized access by configuring and enforcing rules that govern how and when accounts can be used. These policies include enforcing account lockout mechanisms, restricting login times"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1143", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1143 Hidden Window Mitigation", "tags": ["hidden window mitigation", "mitigation", "t1143"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1143 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1143 Whitelist programs that are allowed to have this plist tag. All other programs should be considered suspicious."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1031", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1031 Modify Existing Service Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "modify existing service mitigation", "t1031"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1031 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1031 Use auditing tools capable of detecting privilege and service abuse opportunities on systems within an enterprise and correct them. Limit privileges of user accounts and groups so that only authorized administrators can i"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1041", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1041 Encrypt Sensitive Information", "tags":