://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1406/001 Adversaries may use steganography techniques in order to prevent the detection of hidden information. Steganographic techniques can be used to hide data in digita"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1449", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1449 Exploit SS7 to Redirect Phone Calls/SMS", "tags": ["android", "exploit ss7 to redirect phone calls/sms", "ios", "network-effects", "t1449"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1449 **Tactics:** network-effects **Platforms:** Android, iOS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1449 An adversary could exploit signaling system vulnerabilities to redirect calls or text messages (SMS) to a phone number under the attacker's control. The adversary co"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1628", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1628 Hide Artifacts", "tags": ["android", "defense-evasion", "hide artifacts", "t1628"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1628 **Tactics:** defense-evasion **Platforms:** Android **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1628 Adversaries may attempt to hide artifacts associated with their behaviors to evade detection. Mobile operating systems have features and developer APIs to hide various ar"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1632-001", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1632.001 Code Signing Policy Modification", "tags": ["android", "code signing policy modification", "defense-evasion", "ios", "t1632.001"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1632.001 **Tactics:** defense-evasion **Platforms:** Android, iOS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1632/001 Adversaries may modify code signing policies to enable execution of applications signed with unofficial or unknown keys. Code signing provides a level of aut"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1637-001", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1637.001 Domain Generation Algorithms", "tags": ["android", "command-and-control", "domain generation algorithms", "ios", "t1637.001"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1637.001 **Tactics:** command-and-control **Platforms:** Android, iOS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1637/001 Adversaries may use [Domain Generation Algorithms](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1637/001) (DGAs) to procedurally generate domain names for uses s"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1456", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1456 Drive-By Compromise", "tags": ["android", "drive-by compromise", "initial-access", "ios", "t1456"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1456 **Tactics:** initial-access **Platforms:** Android, iOS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1456 Adversaries may gain access to a system through a user visiting a website over the normal course of browsing. With this technique, the user's web browser is typically"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-defense-evasion", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Defense Evasion", "tags": ["defense evasion", "defense-evasion", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to avoid being detected. Defense evasion consists of techniques an adversary may use to evade detection or avoid other defenses. Sometimes these actions are the same as or variations of techniques in other categories that have the added benefit of subverting a particular defe"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-network-effects", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Network Effects", "tags": ["network effects", "network-effects", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to intercept or manipulate network traffic to or from a device. This category refers to network-based techniques that an adversary may be able to use to fulfill his or her objectives without access to the mobile device itself. These include techniques to intercept or manipula"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-remote-service-effects", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Remote Service Effects", "tags": ["remote service effects", "remote-service-effects", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to control or monitor the device using remote services. This category refers to techniques involving remote services, such as vendor-provided cloud services (e.g. Google Drive, Google Find My Device, or Apple iCloud), or enterprise mobility management (EMM)/mobile device mana"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1006", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1006 Use Recent OS Version", "tags": ["m1006", "mitigation", "use recent os version"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1006 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1006 New mobile operating system versions bring not only patches against discovered vulnerabilities but also often bring security architecture improvements that provide resilience against potential vulnerabilities or weaknesse"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1005", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1005 Application Vetting", "tags": ["application vetting", "m1005", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1005 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1005 Enterprises can vet applications for exploitable vulnerabilities or unwanted (privacy-invasive or malicious) behaviors. Enterprises can inspect applications themselves or use a third-party service. Enterprises may impose "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1012", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1012 Enterprise Policy", "tags": ["enterprise policy", "m1012", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1012 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1012 An enterprise mobility management (EMM), also known as mobile device management (MDM), system can be used to provision policies to mobile devices to control aspects of their allowed behavior."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1011", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1011 User Guidance", "tags": ["m1011", "mitigation", "user guidance"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1011 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1011 Describes any guidance or training given to users to set particular configuration settings or avoid specific potentially risky behaviors."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1059", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1059 Do Not Mitigate", "tags": ["do not mitigate", "m1059", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1059 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1059 This category is to associate techniques that mitigation might increase risk of compromise and therefore mitigation is not recommended."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1058", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1058 Antivirus/Antimalware", "tags": ["antivirus/antimalware", "m1058", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1058 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1058 Mobile security products, such as Mobile Threat Defense (MTD), offer various device-based mitigations against certain behaviors."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1004", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1004 System Partition Integrity", "tags": ["m1004", "mitigation", "system partition integrity"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1004 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1004 Ensure that Android devices being used include and enable the Verified Boot capability, which cryptographically ensures the integrity of the system partition."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1009", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1009 Encrypt Network Traffic", "tags": ["encrypt network traffic", "m1009", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1009 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1009 Application developers should encrypt all of their application network traffic using the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol to ensure protection of sensitive data and deter network-based attacks. If desired, applicat"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1003", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1003 Lock Bootloader", "tags": ["lock bootloader", "m1003", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1003 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1003 On devices that provide the capability to unlock the bootloader (hence allowing any operating system code to be flashed onto the device), perform periodic checks to ensure that the bootloader is locked."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1001", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1001 Security Updates", "tags": ["m1001", "mitigation", "security updates"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1001 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1001 Install security updates in response to discovered vulnerabilities. Purchase devices with a vendor and/or mobile carrier commitment to provide security updates in a prompt manner for a set period of time. Decommission dev"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1010", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1010 Deploy Compromised Device Detection Method", "tags": ["deploy compromised device detection method", "m1010", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1010 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1010 A variety of methods exist that can be used to enable enterprises to identify compromised (e.g. rooted/jailbroken) devices, whether using security mechanisms built directly into the device, third-party mobile security app"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1014", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1014 Interconnection Filtering", "tags": ["interconnection filtering", "m1014", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1014 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1014 In order to mitigate Signaling System 7 (SS7) exploitation, the Communications, Security, Reliability, and Interoperability Council (CSRIC) describes filtering interconnections between network operators to block inappropr"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1007", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1007 Caution with Device Administrator Access", "tags": ["caution with device administrator access", "m1007", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1007 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1007 Warn device users not to accept requests to grant Device Administrator access to applications without good reason. Additionally, application vetting should include a check on whether the application requests Device Admini"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1002", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1002 Attestation", "tags": ["attestation", "m1002", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1002 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1002 Enable remote attestation capabilities when available (such as Android SafetyNet or Samsung Knox TIMA Attestation) and prohibit devices that fail the attestation from accessing enterprise resources."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-software-s0529", "source": "mitre", "title": "S0529 CarbonSteal", "tags": ["carbonsteal", "s0529", "software"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** S0529 **Type:** software **Aliases:** n/a **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0529 [CarbonSteal](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0529) is one of a family of four surveillanceware tools that share a common C2 infrastructure. [CarbonSteal](https://attack.mitre.org/softw"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-software-s0480", "source": "mitre", "title": "S0480 Cerberus", "tags": ["cerberus", "s0480", "software"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** S0480 **Type:** software **Aliases:** n/a **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0480 [Cerberus](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0480) is a banking trojan whose usage can be rented on underground forums and marketplaces. Prior to being available to rent, the authors of ["}, {"id": "mitre-attack-software-s0320", "source": "mitre", "title": "S0320 DroidJack", "tags": ["droidjack", "s0320", "software"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** S0320 **Type:** software **Aliases:** n/a **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0320 [DroidJack](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0320) is an Android remote access tool that has been observed posing as legitimate applications including the Super Mario Run and Pokemon GO "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-software-s0411", "source": "mitre", "title": "S0411 Rotexy", "tags": ["rotexy", "s0411", "software"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** S0411 **Type:** software **Aliases:** n/a **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0411 [Rotexy](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0411) is an Android banking malware that has evolved over several years. It was originally an SMS spyware Trojan first spot