location as to where they can receive an original copy of the Methodology from the Methodology \ncreator. \n4. Any persons who sell training or services of the Methodology must clearly display the name of the \ncreators of this Methodology in addition to the terms of this license. \n5. All persons may include this Methodology in part or in whole in commercial service offerings, private, \ninternal, or non-commercial use including software, checklists, or tools, or within a class or training for  \neducational purposes without explicit consent from the creator providing points 3 and 4 are complied \nwith. \nCreative Commons 3.0 Attribution-Non-Commercial-NoDerivs 2010, ISECOM, www.isecom.org, www.osstmm.org\n208         Official OSSTMM Certifications: www.opsa.org, www.opst.org, www.opse.org, www.owse.org, www.trustanalyst.org\n", "isecom-osstmm-3-119": "# ISECOM OSSTMM v3 — OSSTMM 3 – The Open Source Security Testing Methodology Manual\n\nOSSTMM 3 – The Open Source Security Testing Methodology Manual\n6. No persons may distribute an adaption, modification, or change of this Methodology nor commercially \nsell a product, tool, checklist, or software which applies this Methodology without explicit consent from \nthe creator. \n7. All persons may utilize the Methodology or any portion of it to create or enhance free software and \ncopy and distribute such software under any terms, provided that they also meet all of these conditions: \na) Points 3, 4, 5, and 6 of this License are strictly adhered to. \nb) Any reduction to or incomplete usage of the Methodology in software must strictly and explicitly \nstate which parts of the Methodology were utilized in the software and which parts were not. \nc) When the software is run, all software using the Methodology must either cause the software, when \nstarted running, to print or display an announcement of use of the Methodology including a notice \nof warranty how to view a copy of this License or make clear provisions in another form such as in \ndocumentation or delivered open source code. \n8. If, as a consequence of a court judgment or allegation of Patent infringement, Trade Secret law \ninfringement, or for any other legal reason, where conditions are imposed on any person (whether by \ncourt order, agreement or otherwise) that contradict the conditions of this License, they do not excuse \nsaid person from the conditions of this License. If said person cannot satisfy simultaneously the obligations \nunder this License and any other pertinent obligations, then as a consequence said person may not use, \ncopy, apply, use, distribute, or promote, the Methodology at all. If any portion of this section is held invalid \nor unenforceable under any particular circumstance, the balance of the section is intended to apply and \nthe section as a whole is intended to apply in other circumstances. \n9. If the distribution or use of the Methodology is restricted in certain countries either by patents or by \nTrade Secret interfaces, the original creator who places the Methodology under this License may add an \nexplicit  geographical  distribution  limitation  excluding  those  countries,  so  that  application,  use,  or \ndistribution  is  permitted  only  in  or  among  countries  not  thus  excluded.  In  such  case,  this  License \nincorporates the limitation as if written in the body of this License. \n10. ISECOM may publish revised or new versions of the Open Methodology License. Such new versions will \nbe similar in spirit to the present version, but may differ in detail to address new problems or concerns. \nNO WARRANTY \n11. Because the methodology is licensed free of charge, there is no warranty for the methodology, to the \nextent permitted by applicable law except when otherwise stated in writing the creator or other parties \nprovide the methodology “as is” without a warranty of any kind, either expressed or implied, including, \nbut not limited to, the implied warranties of merchantability and fitness for a particular purpose. The entire \nrisk as to the quality and performance in use of the methodology is with the persons accepting this \nlicense. Should the methodology prove incomplete or incompatible said person assumes the cost of all \nnecessary servicing, repair or correction. \n12.  In no event unless required by applicable law or agreed to in writing will the creator, or any other \nparty who may use, apply, or teach the methodology unmodified as permitted herein, be liable to any \npersons for damages, including any general, special, incidental or consequential damages arising out of \nthe use of or inability to use the methodology (including but not limited to loss, inaccuracies, or failure of  \nthe methodology to operate with any other methodologies), even if such holder or other party has been \nadvised of the possibility of such damages.\nCreative Commons 3.0 Attribution-Non-Commercial-NoDerivs 2010, ISECOM, www.isecom.org, www.osstmm.org\nOfficial OSSTMM Certifications: www.opsa.org, www.opst.org, www.opse.org, www.owse.org, www.trustanalyst.org 209\n", "isecom-osstmm-3-120": "# ISECOM OSSTMM v3 — Why test operations? Unfortunately, not everything works as configured. Not everyone behaves as trained.\n\nWhy test operations? Unfortunately, not everything works as configured. Not everyone behaves as trained.\nTherefore the truth of configuration and training is in the resulting operations. That’s why we need to test\noperations.\nThe Open Source Security Testing Methodology Manual strives to be the ultimate security\ng\nuide.  Better  known  to  security  experts  and  hackers  alike  as  the  OSSTMM,  spoken  like\n“awesome” but with a “t”, is a formal methodology for breaking any security and attacking\nanything the most thorough way possible. \nReleased for free for the first time in 2001 as the underdog to the security industry’s product-\nfocused security advice, the manual achieved an instant following. Being open to anyone for\npeer review and further research led to it growing from its initial 12 page release to its current\nsize of over 200 pages. For testing security operations and devising tactics it has no equal. \nThe OSSTMM is in its third version and is a complete re-write of the original methodology. It now\nincludes the ever-elusive security and trust metrics at its foundation. It required 7 years of\nresearch and development to produce the perfect operational security metric, an algorithm\nwhich computes the Attack Surface of anything. In essence, it is a numerical scale to show\nhow  unprotected  and  exposed  something  currently  is.  Security  professionals,  military\ntacticians, and security researchers know that without knowing how exposed a target is, it’s\njust not possible to say how likely a threat will cause damage and how much. But to know this\nrequires a thorough security test which happens to be exactly what the OSSTMM provides.\nTo say the OSSTMM 3 is a very thorough methodology is an understatement. It covers proper\nattack  procedures,  error  handling,  rules  of  engagement,  proper  analysis,  critical  security\nthinking, and trust metrics. It provides 17 modules like Visibility Audit, Trust Verification, Property\nValidation, and  Competitive  Intelligence  Scouting,  each  which  describes  multiple  attacks\n(called Tasks), for 5 different interaction types with a target (called Channels) organized by\ntechnical knowledge and equipment requirements as Human, Physical, Telecommunications,\nData Networks, and Wireless. The OSSTMM has indeed become a complex organism but with a\nnew focus on readability and usability, it is far from complicated to use. \nSecurity doesn’t have to last forever; just longer than\ne\nverything else that might notice it’s gone.\nOfficial OSSTMM Certifications: www.opsa.org, www.opst.org, \nw\nww.opse.org, www.owse.org, www.trustanalyst.org\nCopyright 2010, ISECOM, www.isecom.org, www.osstmm.org\n", "isecom-cybersecurityplaybook-0": "# ISECOM Cybersecurity Playbook — I S E C O M  P U B L I C A T I O N S\n\nI S E C O M  P U B L I C A T I O N S\nT H E\nOpensource\nCybersecurity\nPlaybook\nISECOM\nInstitute for Security and Open Methodologies\nThe Open Source\nCybersecurity\nPlaybook\nO4 Edition\nWritten by Pete Herzog\nUpdated for OSSTMM 4\nCreative Commons Attribution-NoDerivatives 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM\nContents\nPart 1: Scouting Reports.\nProfiles for the most common threats you should be prepared to face, including the ones that\ndidn't exist when this playbook was first written.\nPart 2: The Game Plan.\nA practical, step-by-step process for making your organization more secure by building\nsecurity in instead of bolting it on.\nPart 3: Looking Downfield.\nSet yourself up for success now and in the long run as threats evolve.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 2\nBefore the Game: How to Read This Playbook\nThe first edition of this playbook treated security as a wall. Put something valuable inside, build\na barrier around it, station somebody to notice when the barrier gets hit. Enough walls, watched\nhard enough, keeps you secure.\nThat's wrong. To know a wall's been breached, something has to watch it, interpret what it saw,\nand respond. Each step costs time and energy. The more you protect and the more connections\nyou have, the faster those costs grow, faster than your budget ever will. You end up spending\nmore to watch more, and always finding out after it happened.\nOSSTMM 4 (O4) is built on a different picture. This edition is organized around it. Three ideas\ncarry it, and you only need the plain-language version to use this book.\nSecurity is shape, not effort. A key doesn't fail to open the wrong lock because turning it\nwould be \"too hard\" or \"take too long.\" It fails because the shapes don't match. No amount of\ntrying gets the attacker in, because there's nothing for the attack to grab onto. Most of what\nfollows is about shaping your organization so that unwanted interactions have nowhere to grab.\nBuilt in, not bolted on. Veterans have said for years that security has to be built in and not\nadded on. O4 is what it looks like when you actually do it.\nRisk already happened. The old definition of risk was a bet: the probability of a bad future\nevent times how much it would hurt. A gambling metric. But you can't react faster than the\nspeed of cause and effect. By the time your sensor notices an event, decides what it means,\nand responds, the event is already in the past and its consequences have already spread.\n\"Detect and respond\" is really \"high-speed forensics.\" You arrive just in time to document what\nalready happened. So stop thinking of risk as a future bet. Risk is a present-tense fact: how\nmuch of your organization is reachable right now. That's your attack surface, and you can\nmeasure it, shrink it, and audit it today.\nYou win in the Intent phase. Every interaction has three parts: before contact (Intent), during\ncontact (React), and after contact (Resolve). Since you can't out-run the speed of cause and\neffect once contact happens, React and Resolve are mostly forensics. The one place a\ndefender genuinely wins is before contact, reading the attacker's reach while it's still forming.\nThe scan before the hit. The reconnaissance before the con. The update that quietly changed\nwhat it does before you installed it. Reading intent before it lands is the closest thing in security\nto seeing the future, and this edition treats it as a first-class part of the game plan.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 3\nPart 1: Scouting Reports\nWhat security threats should I be prepared for?\nThe first key to any effective game plan is knowing what you're up against. In this section you'll\nlearn about the most common threats your company is likely to face.\nThis list is not comprehensive, and it isn't meant to be. It's meant to show you the tactics being\ndirected at you and your users, and the specific reasons you're vulnerable to each, because\nonce you can see the reason, you can change the shape that creates it. As you read, watch for\ntwo things under every threat: where it first reaches toward you (its Intent phase, the part you\ncan catch early) and what trust it's abusing to work (the coupling you can remove).\nFrom phishing to ransomware to tainted software updates, the more you know about these\nthreats the better. They're some of the leading causes of data breaches, downtime, and a\nserious lack of sleep.\nPhishing\nWhat it is: Any attempt to compromise a system or steal information by tricking a user into\nresponding to a malicious message. The most common phishing attacks involve emails armed\nwith malware hidden in attachments or links to infected websites, though phishing also travels\nby voicemail, text message, and social media.\nWhat makes protection a challenge: Employees are already in the habit of clicking things,\nbecause that's how you interact with modern computers. Phishing emails are far more\nconvincing than they used to be. Scammers take over legitimate accounts or spoof addresses\nso a message looks like it came from someone the employee trusts. Once a victim is\ncompromised, the attacker has their access: the same servers, web apps, and files, as if they\nwere that employee. Protecting against it means limiting what employees can reach and how,\nwhich can run counter to what they need to do their jobs. And the most targeted \"spear\nphishing\" gives no red flags until it's too late.\nPhishing almost always has a warm-up you can see if you're looking. A lookalike domain\nregistered last week. A first contact that breaks a known routine (a \"vendor\" who emails when\nthey always call). A reply-to that doesn't match the sender. That's the Intent phase showing\nitself before the payload lands.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 4\nSocial Engineering\nWhat it is: There are two ways to steal anything: take it yourself, or get someone else to hand it\nto you. Social engineering is the umbrella term for any tactic that exploits and manipulates trust\nso the victim gives the attacker what they want. Access to information, accounts, or computers\ninside a secured area. Think a fake customer-service call to reset a password, or a criminal\nspoofing your CEO's email and asking finance for an urgent wire transfer, a scam known as\nbusiness email compromise (BEC).\nWhat makes protection a challenge: Everyone, repeat, everyone, can be conned, defrauded,\nfooled, or manipulated. Sometimes it's a lack of training; more often it's distraction and mental\nfatigue. Because this attack targets people directly, technical safeguards can do very little,\nespecially when the request isn't outside the employee's normal responsibilities, like resetting a\npassword for a desperate user. Social engineering never breaks a lock; it borrows the authority\nof someone the target already trusts.\nRansomware\nWhat it is: Malicious software that encrypts a victim's files and demands payment, usually in\nanonymous cryptocurrency, to decrypt them. Like other malware, ransomware usually starts\nwith a phishing email or a compromised website. Unlike other malware, its goal isn't stealth and\npersistence. It's to spread as fast as possible, encrypt as much as possible, and then loudly\nannounce itself so the criminals can extort you.\nWhat makes protection a challenge: Ransomware locks up any drive the employee can reach,\nincluding connected USB drives and network shares. Once files are encrypted, your only\noptions are a reliable, current backup; a researcher-released decryptor if you're lucky; or\nholding your nose and paying, which is anything but a sure thing. The authors are criminals and\noccasionally bad at coding, so you can pay and still lose the files. Worst of all, ransomware\nturns a strength, files being accessible across the organization, into the weakness. It's a billion-\ndollar industry now, which means it changes faster than signature-based tools can keep up.\nNote that ransomware's blast radius is exactly the reach of the account it lands on. The attack\ndidn't decide that radius. Your zoning did, months earlier.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 5\nDownloaders\nWhat it is: Normal-looking programs designed to fetch and install malware without tripping\nalarms. A downloader is the attacker's \"man on the inside.\" It slips in first, scopes out the\nsecurity settings, then smuggles the dangerous payload once it's decided the coast is clear.\nEven after the payload is found and removed, a downloader left hiding can start the whole\nprocess over.\nWhat makes protection a challenge: Downloaders are one step removed from the dirty work,\nso they don't carry the functionality that gets malware blocked. Their makers can focus entirely\non evading detection. Think of it as attackers fielding skilled specialists instead of mediocre\ngeneralists: the downloader is a prolific passer, the payload a sensational scorer, and paired up\nthey're far more effective than either alone.\nDrive-by Downloads / Download Hijacking\nWhat it is: In nature, big predators wait at the watering hole for prey to come to them. On the\nInternet they turn popular website visits into covert attacks. Injecting code through comments\nthat force automatic downloads, compromising a web server to taint legitimate downloads, or\nrunning exploit kits that probe each visitor's system for a vulnerability to hit.\nWhat makes protection a challenge: Attackers have surprise and a deep bag of tricks. Update\nyour browser and they update their code; patch one hole and they move to the next. And it isn't\nonly sketchy sites. Some of the web's most popular destinations have been compromised. You\nusually can't ask employees to just stop using the Internet.\nMalvertising\nWhat it is: Marketers aren't the only ones who use advertising to get in front of crowds.\nCriminals create fake ads, or slip malicious code into legitimate ones, so they can literally\ncapture their audience.\nWhat makes protection a challenge: Online advertising is everywhere and getting more\naggressive, and people have stopped viewing ads, even pop-ups, with much suspicion. The\nknee-jerk fix is ad-blocker software, but many sites refuse to work with it on. And if employees\nhave to choose between their ad-blocker and a top-10 list of cat videos, well.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 6\nZero-Day Attack\nWhat it is: Traditionally, a zero-day is an undisclosed vulnerability attackers can exploit before\nanyone, victim or vendor, knows to patch it. The term also gets stretched to cover attacks using\nbrand-new tactics, exploits, or malware variants that haven't been seen before.\nWhat makes protection a challenge: It's hard to defend against something you've never\nencountered, especially when it blindsides you. Signature-based tools are particularly weak\nhere, because they identify malware by matching it against a list of samples they've already\nseen, and a never-before-seen exploit has no match. Because zero-days work, they're in high\ndemand, and patching one hole often buys only momentary relief before attackers move to the\nnext. You cannot enumerate every unknown attack, so stop trying to recognize the attack and\nstart removing the surface it would need. A payload with nowhere to execute and no path to\nreach is defeated whether you've ever seen it before or not.\nPassword Cracking\nWhat it is: A login and password isn't what most people think it is. It's a complicated chain of\nprocesses: multiple systems, secure transport to and from servers, a trusted network of server\nidentity assurance and revocation, code to judge password complexity, more code to confirm a\nhuman is typing, a second factor, and a way to recover lost passwords. So password cracking\nis rarely just guessing the password; it's cracking the process to take over the account.\nWhat makes protection a challenge: Any system that lets users in from anywhere and asks\nthem to create, safeguard, and remember their own passwords is going to be hard (maybe\nimpossible) to defend. O4 names the reason directly: the more a person is placed inside a\nsecurity process, the larger the reachable surface of that process becomes. (Longtime\nOSSTMM readers knew this as \"the Somebody Sequence.\") Asking employees to manage their\nown passwords is like handing out copies of the key to an important lock. You can buy the\nstrongest lock money can buy, but how secure is it with keys floating around everywhere? The\nfix isn't a bigger lock. It's taking the person back out of the process wherever you can (single\nsign-on, hardware keys, certificates), so there's less surface for cracking to reach.\nDistributed Denial of Service (DDoS)\nWhat it is: A system can only process so much before it slows and buckles. By commandeering\na large number of hijacked systems and devices (a botnet), attackers aim a flood of connection\nrequests or junk packets at one target to overload it and knock it offline.\nWhat makes protection a challenge: The bigger the botnet, the more damage. Your best hope\nunder attack is a capable anti-DDoS service, and even that's no guarantee against a high-\nmagnitude flood. Worse, attackers sometimes contact targets in advance and threaten to knock\nthem offline unless \"protection money\" is paid, and as with ransomware, paying criminals never\ncomes with a guarantee.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 7\nScareware\nWhat it is: You've seen the pop-ups: \"Warning! A virus has been detected. Download\nVirusBlaster to remove it.\" The real infection is the program the pop-up is trying to get you to\ndownload. Scareware comes dressed as fake antivirus, fake browsers, or fake updates.\nWhat makes protection a challenge: Social engineering works because it preys on the\ndistracted and fatigued, and scareware adds eagerness to help into the mix, the \"good\nintentions\" spiral that leads to bad decisions. Once scareware is installed it inherits all the\nprivileges, passwords, and logins of the employee who ran it. Getting it out might be as easy as\nwiping and restoring from backup. If it has spread, much harder.\nSQL Injection\nWhat it is: If a website has an input box or form (a login, a credit-card field), an attacker can try\nfeeding it structured query language code to read or alter the stored data behind it.\nWhat makes protection a challenge: SQL injection abuses a trust between the web application\nand its database to do nearly anything it wants. If all you picture is \"delete data,\" you're\nunderestimating it: add, change, and remove data; steal credit-card numbers, personal data,\nand health records; or insert malicious code that gets served back to your own users. From\nthere criminals can turn your popular site into their tool: distributing drive-by downloads,\nbuilding botnets, even hijacking DNS to send visitors to malicious lookalikes of sites they trust.\nIf the login form is vulnerable, injection can bypass the login entirely. Any user-input field on a\ndatabase-backed site is potentially injectable, which makes it widespread. And you can't just\nremove all input and still take orders or feedback. The durable fix is structural: the application\nshould treat input as data that can never be executed as a command, so the coupling injection\ndepends on simply isn't there.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 8\nTainted Updates & AI-Generated Code NEW FOR O4\nWhat it is: The threat that grew up since this playbook was first written. Software no longer\narrives in shrink-wrap; it pours across your perimeter every day: vendor updates, browser\nextensions, open-source packages, marketplace apps, and now code and scripts generated on\nthe spot by AI assistants and agents. Attackers have learned that the easiest way past your\ndefenses is to ride in on something you already trust: a legitimate package whose latest version\nwas quietly poisoned, an extension that changed hands, a dependency buried four levels deep,\nan AI-generated script nobody reviewed. This is a supply-chain and software-perimeter attack,\nand it's now one of the most common ways organizations get hit.\nWhat makes protection a challenge: Nobody signs off. Gigabytes of code cross the perimeter\nthrough dozens of channels, far faster than any human can review, and most of it is barely\nlooked at, if at all. The core mistake is treating trust as if it carries across versions: you\napproved this vendor once, so every future update is fine; this package was clean last month,\nso it's clean today. It isn't. The malicious change is almost always in the update, not the original.\nAnd AI now writes new internal apps and scripts faster than manual review can keep up, so the\npile grows while the review shrinks. The answer isn't a blanket ban. Your business runs on this\nsoftware. The answer is to read each thing's intent at the gate, every version, before it runs.\nThat's Part 2's intent scanning, and this is the threat it was built for.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 9\nPart 2: The Game Plan\nAs anyone who's been on the wrong side of a data breach can attest, security is not a game.\nThe stakes are real, and unlike a game there are no set rules or boundaries, and attackers aren't\nknown for fair play.\nStill, we talk about security in sports terms. There's an offense and a defense. The defender's\ngoal is to stop the attacker from reaching or damaging our assets, using defensive tactics the\nway the attacker uses offensive ones.\nThe hard part is that we don't know the parameters of the \"game\" until it's too late. We don't\nknow who the opponent is, what they can do, or what they want. Stealthy robbery or a smash-\nand-grab to encrypt and ransom? Are we a target of hate or a moment of opportunity? So we're\npressured to build tactics that work against a huge, fast-changing variety of attacks, while also\nkeeping the business running. We don't just have to stop attacks; we have to do it without\nslowing or stopping the work.\nAn attacker has no such limits. They only have to be effective against one part of our defense,\nby any means, to win. We have to be effective against all of it, under budgets, laws, and\nregulations. The field is anything but level.\nWith the game stacked like that, what does a winning strategy actually look like? It is not trying\nto out-watch and out-react an opponent who is faster, cheaper, and only needs to be right\nonce. That's a race you lose by playing. The winning strategy uses the two advantages the\nattacker can't take away: you can shape the field so attacks have nowhere to land (build\nsecurity in), and you can read the attacker's reach before contact (scan for intent). Everything\nbelow is one of those two.\nCreate a Zone Defense\nYou can't go one-on-one with an opponent you can't see coming. To counter an attacker's\nflexibility and surprise, build a zone defense. In sports, a zone separates the field into areas of\nfocused defense; the idea is that by creating separation you ensure a threat either cannot\nphysically reach an asset or meets a wall of defense when it tries.\nSeparation is the whole game, and it's the first thing O4 makes you build in rather than watch.\nEvery connection you allow is a path an attack can travel, and every trust you grant is a\ncoupling an attack can borrow. A zone defense shrinks your reachable surface (how much of\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 10\nyou is in reach right now) by removing paths and couplings you never actually needed. You're\nnot adding a guard to watch the door; you're taking the door out of the wall where no one\nneeds to walk through it. Build your game plan around the following:\nSeparate services and assets into zones, logically and physically. Assets of one\ndepartment should be reachable only by that department. Whitelist all system access\n(limit it to a predetermined list of authorized types) through a switch and a firewall. Every\npath you don't explicitly need is reach you're giving away.\n1.\nZone your remote (vendor and cloud) accounts with a separate login and secret\nsentence for each. Re-using passwords across vendors is the fastest way to get\naccounts compromised: one breach becomes all of them. Personal certificates and\nprivate-key cryptography are much stronger than even secret sentences, and should be\nused for administrative (root) access to external services. Protect even those keys with a\nsecret sentence: a full sentence with capitalization and punctuation that means something\nto you, so you'll remember it. (For Facebook: I just wasted 2 hours on Facebook! For your\nVoIP provider: Boss says, \"No more big bills.\" Punctuation included.)\n2.\nCreate trust zones where no system can directly reach another. Desktops shouldn't\nconnect to other desktops; network shares should be closed. Even on the same network,\nno system should reach another unless you planned for it. Have desktops connect to a\nfileserver to store documents. Make sure administrative ports (SSH on servers, admin\nweb access on intranet servers) are reachable only from the administrator's system, or\nbetter, only at that server's own keyboard if it's local. This is least privilege at the network\nlevel: only those who should reach a document, printer, file server, or resource can.\n3.\nCreate system zones, not just people zones. When you authorize who can access what,\nyou've granted a trust, and trust is a coupling attacks borrow. Phishing works precisely by\nriding a trusted user's access to reach other systems with the user none the wiser. So\nverify that a resource is being reached by the authorized person, not silently from their\ncompromised system. CAPTCHA is one common way to force proof of a human; it also\ncuts web-comment spam and brute-force password attempts.\n4.\nAvoid all-in-one networked systems (NAS + printer, router + wi-fi). They're inherently\nweaker because they're harder to configure securely and harder to zone by who's\nauthorized. If your router has wi-fi built in, disable it and use a separate wi-fi router in a\nzone that can't reach internal systems.\n5.\nKeep a rigorous patching schedule for anything directly on the Internet. Racing to patch\nshouldn't be your primary defense, but for Internet-facing services it's one of the few\nprotections you have. For non-Internet-facing systems, fold patching into regular\nmaintenance rather than auto-installing everything the moment it ships. That gives you\ntime to test on a standard system first and builds a routine in your traffic that makes a\nmalicious install stand out. (And read Part 1's new scouting report: an update is not\nautomatically safe just because the last version was. Scan each one's intent before\nrollout; see below.)\n6.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 11\nZone your desktops, servers, and mobile devices to separate what can execute from\nwhat can read/write. Let temp files be created while denying them the ability to execute,\nin case of malware. On desktops and mobile devices, whitelist exactly which applications\nmay run or use the network and deny the rest. Exercise least privilege so users don't get\nadministrative rights by default.\n7.\nKeep work and personal mobile devices separate. App security is notoriously bad, and\neach app on a device is connected to every other app on it, which makes zoning\nessential. If your business requires mobile devices, separate which may hold personal\napps and which hold work apps. There's technology to keep work and personal data\napart on one physical device if you'd rather not carry two.\n8.\nZone with multiple web browsers. Keep one browser clean, no plug-ins, strictly for\nbanking, vendor administration, and other critically confidential connections. Use another\nfor online shopping. Use another for news, surfing, and social; do run ad blockers and\nprivacy plug-ins on that one to minimize malicious-ad exposure. A single browser with\nmultiple profiles can work too, though some browsers make you close one profile to open\nanother.\n9.\nZone your remote interactions: remote access to the office, cloud services, web-based\nvendor administration, email. Your best protection here is encryption. Use an encrypted\nVPN whenever you're sending or receiving from somewhere that isn't your place of work.\nBecause email is plain text and readable along its route, use email encryption anytime\nyou're mailing between remote offices or sending sensitive information.\n10.\nUse work hours as part of your zoning. Shut down systems, routers, and wi-fi you don't\nneed running at the end of the day, on weekends, and on holidays. If you can't staff\nincident response during those times, those things shouldn't be connected to the Internet\nduring them. A system that isn't reachable isn't attackable.\n11.\nUse blacklists if you can't whitelist, or alongside whitelisting. A blacklist names what's \nnot allowed; a whitelist names what is. Blacklists let you deny whole categories of\ndevices, programs, or service types you don't need. They're weaker and easier to bypass\nthan whitelists, but much easier to implement and maintain, which makes them useful \nadditional separation. Most antivirus, spam control, and firewall geo-blocking runs on\nblacklists. Just never make a blacklist your main form of separation; attackers find it\nshockingly easy to step around.\n12.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 12\nThe zone-defense mindset, in O4 terms: every item above either removes a path or\nremoves a trust. You're shrinking the part of your organization that's reachable right now,\nwhich is the only honest definition of reducing risk. And notice what you didn't do: you didn't\npost a guard on every interaction to watch it forever. Constant watching doesn't scale. The\ncost of checking everything, all the time, grows with the square of the connections. Double\nthe parts and you roughly quadruple the watching bill, until you're spending more energy\npolicing the system than running it. Biology solved this three billion years ago: your body\ndoesn't interrogate all 37 trillion cells from a central office; each cell governs itself and\nquietly shuts down if it goes wrong. Good zoning is the same move. Build each zone to\ngovern itself, to deny by default, to need no outside referee for the normal case, and you get\nsecurity that holds without a watcher you can't afford.\nScan for Intent\nEverything that's going to hit you reaches toward you first. The scan comes before the break-in.\nThe lookalike domain gets registered before the phishing email. The downloader checks the\nlocks before the payload arrives. The poisoned update changes what it does before you install\nit. All of that is the Intent phase (the \"before contact\"), and it's the one window where a\ndefender genuinely beats the attacker, because once contact lands you're into React and\nResolve, and those are just forensics at the speed of light. If you only instrument one new\ncapability from this edition, instrument this one.\nScanning for intent means reading what something is about to do instead of waiting to see what\nit did. Two fronts matter most for a small or mid-sized business, and you should shape your\ntools around both.\nRead the reach aimed at your people and your network. Attackers footprint before they strike.\nMake that footprinting expensive and noisy so it shows itself:\nChange how your servers respond to queries. Attackers mapping you for a targeted\nattack are mostly trying to learn what systems and applications you run. Change your\nservers' responses so there are only positives and false positives. Flood the attacker's\ntools with noise so they can't tell a real reply from a fake one. Set web servers to answer\nevery request with a 200 even when the honest answer is 404 or 302, and common\nscraping and analysis tools go mostly useless. Tune TCP so every port answers every\nSYN with an ACK (easiest with a port-forwarding firewall answering for the server\nwhether one exists at that address or not) and a port scan becomes worthless. It won't\nstop an advanced attacker with better tools (that's what your zone defense is for), but it\nshrinks the pool of attackers who can get anywhere, and it turns their reconnaissance into\nyour early warning.\n1.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 13\nRead the intent of software before you let it run. This is the newer and now larger front, and\nit's where \"intent scanning\" becomes concrete. Software crosses your perimeter all day:\ndownloads, browser extensions, vendor updates, open-source packages, marketplace apps,\nand AI-generated code and scripts. Most organizations let it in on reputation (\"we trust this\nvendor\") and never look again. That's the gap. What you need is a single control point at every\nplace software enters that reads each artifact's intent before it runs, and then approves the\nsafe, blocks the clearly bad, and escalates the edge cases to a human. Shape your tooling so\nthat, at the gate, it can:\nKnow what normal looks like, so abnormal announces itself. This is situational\nawareness as an instrument. Know your employees, vendors, routines, hours, and policies\nwell enough that a deviation is loud. No \"Microsoft Security Center\" should be calling to\nremotely inspect a system you never bought a service for. Knowing that, an employee\nrecognizes the social-engineering warm-up and stops it in the Intent phase. Changes in\nroutine (a customer who emails when they always call) are among the best early signals\nyou have. Make it routine for employees to escalate and document those changes; later\nyou can correlate them with patterns on your network, and you'll often find the\nreconnaissance that preceded an attempt.\n2.\nWatch for the scan before the hit. Your network monitoring should treat a port scan, a\nsweep of your admin ports, or a sudden probe of a rarely-touched service as what it is:\nsomeone reading your shape before they act. Move an obvious target like SSH off its\ndefault port and an automated scan looking for it both misses and trips your sensor.\nYou've converted the attacker's own Intent-phase move into your alert.\n3.\nRead provenance. Where did this actually come from, who owns it now, and does its\norigin match what it claims? A package that quietly changed hands is a flag.\n•\nRead behavior against known tactics. Does this artifact's behavior line up with\nrecognized attacker techniques (reaching for credentials, phoning home, escalating\nprivilege, hiding itself) before it's allowed to execute? Catch the intent in the code, not the\ndamage on the network.\n•\nRead the change, not just the thing. This is the single most important habit: trust does\nnot carry across versions. An update is a brand-new artifact that happens to share a\nname with one you trusted. Read whether its risk went up from the version before (new\ncapabilities, new network destinations, new embedded code) and treat a risk jump as a\nstop-and-look, not a routine install.\n•\nRead for tomorrow's weaknesses. Surface quantum-unsafe cryptography and other\naging-out assurances now, while a planned migration is cheap, instead of discovering\nthem under a compliance deadline or after they break.\n•\nRead AI-generated artifacts like any other software. Code and scripts written by an AI\nassistant or agent are software crossing your perimeter, and they deserve the same gate\n•\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 14\nThe point of all of this is advantage, not more alarms. A gate that reads intent at the place\nsoftware enters, at zero distance, once, before the thing couples to you, is dramatically cheaper\nthan watching every endpoint forever and cleaning up after each install. That's the same self-\ngovernance move as good zoning: push the decision to the boundary and make it there, rather\nthan funding a demon to watch everything downstream. Do this well and approvals that used to\ntake weeks take minutes, and the poisoned thing never gets the chance to run. That's what it\nmeans to see it coming.\nApply Rehabilitation Tactics\nAs in sports, outcomes in security are often decided by conditioning done far in advance.\nAthletes don't show up on game day without countless hours of practice, and franchises don't\ninvest in star players without first hiring the trainers, doctors, and coaches to keep them\nhealthy. Ask yourself: how rigorously has your security been tested? How many scenarios have\nyou prepared for? How fast can you recover from a setback?\nIn O4 terms, this is about continuity and resilience: making sure that when contact does land\n(and some always will), the Resolve phase is a quick bounce-back instead of a slow bleed.\nSecurity is equal parts preparation and recovery. Adjust your game plan to regularly revisit what\nto do when something goes wrong:\nas a vendor package: same questions, same approve/block/escalate. \"An AI wrote it\" is\nnot provenance; it's a reason to look harder.\nSet up a way to back up and restore systems, data, mobile devices, and desktops.\nBackups should be centralized, secured, and ideally encrypted. If they're encrypted, both\noff-site (cloud) and internal backups are acceptable, but remember, anything backed up\noutside your network is no longer in your control. If you can't afford to properly build and\ntest a backup system, you're better off leaving it to a service that can. The key is quick\nrecovery: test your restores and make sure they happen fast.\n1.\nKeep tools handy to fully image a drive for forensics and recovery. Both hardware and\nsoftware options exist, some certified for criminal investigations. If you carry cyber\ninsurance, you may be required to use specific forensic software to file a claim.\n2.\nKeep shared data in central locations like a NAS server. That limits the loss when one\ndesktop fails. Standardized desktop installs plus nightly backups shorten recovery\nfurther. A well-prepared shop can fully image a compromised system for forensics, wipe\nit, and reinstall to near-full operation in under two hours.\n3.\nBack up everything, even your centralized data. It's the only way to assure a complete\nrecovery. Set all data services and file servers to be backed up and redundant with fall-\nback servers, so a server failure doesn't mean loss of service. This is continuity built in.\n4.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 15\nUse Your Home Field Advantage\nThe Internet is a hostile environment, which is exactly why we go to such lengths to let our\nnetworks interact while staying separated from it. But don't stop at making a safe place for your\nusers; go one step further and make it a hostile place for attackers.\nCreating conditions that let you thrive while your opponent struggles is what sports calls home-\nfield advantage. Sometimes it's just the crowd; sometimes it's real environmental factors. Some\nteams are built for grass over turf, altitude, heat, or cold. In cybersecurity your three home-field\nadvantages are visibility, protocol, and process. Lean on them and you force the attacker to\nplay the away team: they have to come into an environment you know better than anyone alive.\nIn O4 terms, all three come from knowing your own system's healthy ground state so well that\nanything off-pattern stands out.\nKeep emergency contact numbers for vendor support. If forensics uncovers why an\nattack succeeded, it may need immediate attention (a patch, a configuration change, or\nboth), and you don't want to be hunting for a phone number mid-incident.\n5.\nConsider a distributed denial of service (DDoS) protection service, especially if your\nbusiness depends on customers reaching an online presence like a store. Many hosts\noffer this already; make sure you know how much protection they actually provide, and\nconsider a specialist if you need more.\n6.\nKnow what's happening on your network. This is probably your single biggest home-\nfield advantage. To see what's running at any hour, there are tools worth looking into.\nSIEM products consolidate logs and real-time traffic; intrusion detection systems\n(generally a blacklist technology) watch live traffic and respond to some attacks. Even\nwithout them, free tools like tcpdump and Wireshark give you more than enough to\nunderstand what's on your network and where it's going, enough to see whether your\nzone defense is working. As the network grows, behavioral-analysis tools help automate\nsniffing out anomalies, because not all malicious activity is obvious.\n1.\nKnow what belongs on each default install, every desktop and server. That lets you\nrecognize malware or non-standard installs on sight. File-integrity software will alert you\nto changes in files and directories that signal compromise, and it's most powerful on\nservers, where it can immediately replace files an attacker dropped.\n2.\nChange default settings. Your protocols aren't just the packets on your network, but how\nyou've configured them to run. An attacker has to learn what you run, how it's connected,\nand how it responds, so change the default install and directory names, default logins and\npasswords, and default configurations like the service ports on devices and servers. That\nalone protects you from the bots that hunt for defaults. Simply moving SSH's admin port\nto a random high number keeps it out of obvious reach and lets your monitoring catch the\nscan that comes looking: an Intent-phase win handed to you for free.\n3.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 16\nChange how your servers respond to queries. (This is important enough that it leads the\nScan for Intent section above. It belongs in both, because it's both a home-field\nadvantage and your cheapest early-warning system.) Flooding attack tools with positives\nand false positives turns reconnaissance into noise for them and signal for you.\n4.\nKnow what normal looks like by knowing and understanding your employees, vendors,\nroutines, operating hours, and policies. If you know no \"Microsoft Security Center\" should\never call to inspect your systems, you'll recognize the call for the social-engineering\nattempt it is and stop it fast.\n5.\nMake your users part of the solution. When employees know what's normal, they\nprevent the majority of fraud and phishing. People who know the routine notice fast when\na customer emails instead of calls, and a change in routine is one of the best attack\nindicators you have, with employees as your first line by reporting it. Make escalating and\ndocumenting those changes routine, and you build a forensic trail you can later correlate\nwith network patterns. Get in the habit of proactively asking employees how things are\nrunning. A noisy drive, a slow system, or a strange message can then be investigated\nimmediately, and if you're set up for quick recovery, a suspicious system can be wiped\nand reinstalled before it becomes a crisis.\n6.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 17\nPart 3: Looking Downfield\nDespite the sports analogy, let's be clear: cybersecurity isn't something you dabble in. There's\nno bush-league cybersecurity. It's not a game. Losers are real victims with real, sometimes\ncatastrophic losses, and even winning is a fleeting moment, gone as fast as it came.\nCybersecurity is hard because we don't know all the vulnerabilities we have or all the threats\nthere may be, and even if we did, they change too fast to hold a specific defense against each\none. The environment itself keeps changing: new technologies, new business models, new\nservices, new communication channels, and now AI writing software faster than anyone can\nreview it. This competition with attackers lasts indefinitely, so your strategy has to win not just\nthis season but every season after.\nTo do that, keep your players in shape, steadily improve your equipment, maintain a healthy\nenvironment, and employ the right tactics. This is also where O4 earns its keep: because you've\nbuilt security into the shape of your organization rather than bolting watchers onto it, you're not\nre-fighting every new threat from scratch. A smaller reachable surface and a habit of reading\nintent keep working against attacks that hadn't been invented when you set them up.\nTraining\nAll employees need to understand how cybersecurity touches their day-to-day role, and they\nneed training in situational awareness both on and off the computer.\nTeach all employees how they can be manipulated and fooled, in person and online. There\nare infinite variations of an attack but a finite number of ways a person is vulnerable to fraud\nand manipulation. Rather than expecting employees to identify every shifting threat, teach them\nhow to react securely when one of their vulnerabilities is probed. For example, when they're\nasked for sensitive information. You're training the Intent-phase reflex: recognize the reach and\nrespond safely before contact.\nMake it easy for employees to talk about security or privacy concerns. Employees are your\nbest anomaly-detection engine. Make sure they can voice doubts and report unusual activity\nbefore those concerns grow into real problems.\nReally know your systems. Anyone in IT or security should know how all the current software\nand systems actually work inside your organization, not just be certified or experienced in the\nbrand. You can't secure what you don't understand in your own environment. Bring in a coach\nwho'll teach you and your team security in the context of your operations.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 18\nUnderstand how to apply the basics. All security personnel should be well versed in the\noperational security fundamentals of O4, which apply anywhere from physical to cyber to\nanything. Don't let them just say they read it. Have them map your business processes onto\nO4's measurements and controls so you can see, concretely, how much of your organization is\nreachable right now. That reachable surface is your attack surface, and putting a number on it\nturns \"are we secure?\" from a guess into something you can audit, shrink, and re-check.\nExercises\nKnowing how to swim means nothing when the tsunami hits. To stay above hostile waters, train\nregularly on how to react when things go wrong, so that despite fear, confusion, or fatigue, the\ndefault reaction is the secure one.\nCreate an incident response plan and drill it. Build an emergency checklist employees can\nfollow and keep walking through it until everyone's clear on how to react. Include new hires\nfrom day one.\nGet employees used to dealing with attacks. Run regular exercises designed to manipulate\nthem: mock phishing, in-person and phone social engineering, fake malware activity. Make the\nIntent-phase moment familiar so it's recognized under pressure.\nGive short, mandatory security challenges and puzzles. Keep them to a couple of minutes,\ndone without pulling employees fully out of their day. This trains them to think security while\nworking, because nobody gets to stop working during a real attack.\nEquipment\nIn many sports, being the better athlete doesn't guarantee a win; the difference between first\nand second often comes down to equipment: new materials, new technology, new designs.\nMake the most of your tech. Better routers improve response times, better processors allow\nstronger encryption, and more elastic monitoring reduces how thin you have to spread\nresources.\nConsider the cloud. Cloud services can give you functionality you don't have to build or run\nyourself. There's a real trade-off in control and self-sufficiency (what's off your network is\noutside your control), but the added capability can let you do more with less.\nWhen you can't buy new, maintain the old. Sometimes upgrading RAM or disks is enough to\nmake the difference.\nKeep your systems running lean. Stick to necessary software, minimizing the memory-\nresident, memory-hungry programs that slowly grind a system to a halt. Lean systems also have\na smaller reachable surface: less installed is less to attack.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 19\nEquip yourself to read intent. The modern addition to the kit: make sure your tooling can\nactually read what crosses your perimeter before it runs. Software at the gate and\nreconnaissance on the wire. You don't need a specific product name; you need the capability,\nbuilt in at the points software and traffic actually enter, so the decision happens at the boundary\ninstead of after the fact.\nEnvironment\nSome athletes compete on clay, some on grass, some on turf. What they share is that they've\nhoned their game to their environment and built a home-field advantage by learning to\noutcompete others on it. Do the same by getting to know your own environment, actively\nmaintaining it and noticing even the smallest changes.\nKnow what \"normal\" looks like. Routinely examine the protocols on the network, which\nsystems talk to each other, how employees use their desktops, and how mobile devices\nbehave. Fire up a packet sniffer and watch live traffic in different segments; make sure you\nknow what all of it is and whether it should even be there. A constant-monitoring tool like a\nSIEM is better still. Then track down the anomalies. Knowing normal is what makes abnormal\nvisible. It's the ground state everything else is measured against.\nRemember: not all software needs to be updated. Always update when a security patch is\nnecessary and you have no other way to control the vulnerability. That requires the people and\ntime to investigate. If an update is purely new functionality, ask whether you actually need it.\nMany a process has broken and many a file been lost to an update that changed something you\ndidn't need. As any coach will tell you, if it's not broken, don't fix it. So have a process to pick\nand choose which updates get installed. And when you do install one, read its intent first (Part\n2): a trusted name on a new version is not the same as a trusted version.\nMake rounds and talk to employees. Learn what they need to do their jobs, how they deal with\nthird-party vendors, and what software they use. People build routines, and they're your best\nsource for knowing when something outside your direct control (cloud services, vendor\nextranets) stops behaving normally.\nForget Strategy, Choose Your Tactics\nA strategy is part of any modern business plan. So what's a cybersecurity strategy? A plan with\ngoals and objectives to achieve cybersecurity.\nPeople who sell cybersecurity strategies like to say it also includes specifics on tools and\nmetrics. But that's just smuggling tactics into the strategy so it doesn't sound so useless.\nYes, useless. Fun fact: a cybersecurity strategy is useless. If you don't have one written down,\nit's because you already have one so obvious you never bothered, the same way you don't\nhave a formal not dying strategy. A formal one would say you don't want threats of any sort\naffecting your assets of any sort, now or in the future. Which is obvious.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 20\nSo why the obsession with strategy? Because tactics are hard. It's easier and safer to make a\nstrategy sound important while meaning nothing than to choose and implement tactics that\nactually work, and you look good longer, because an empty strategy can coast for ages while\nempty tactics get noticed immediately.\nTactics are where the rubber meets the road. They're the bat striking the ball; they're literally\nthe packets smacking the server. They're how you \"do the thing you do to the things you have\"\nto achieve security, and that's hard to plan out. In O4 terms, every good tactic is a control\napplied at zero distance, built into the place where the interaction happens, not watched from\nacross a gap. A few steps to point you the right way:\nDraw out the interactions and separations between your systems. Base it on the packet\nactivity you actually see on the network: a hybrid physical-and-logical map with arrows for the\ninteractions. Then decide what should be separated or controlled. That's you applying tactics.\nList all of your network processes: server backups, administration, remote desktop support,\nand the rest. Know exactly what systems and people each can interact with, then decide what it\nshould interact with. How you control that authorization is you applying tactics.\nDo the same for wireless, physical room and asset access, and telephone systems. Decide\nwhich tactics you need to assure control.\nThen take a step back. Make sure every tactic you chose fits the overall company resources\nand doesn't hamper workflow. Security that stops the business isn't security; it's just a different\noutage.\nLearn to Work with Non-Technical Colleagues\nHumans are social animals. Most of us like being liked, belonging to a team, standing up for a\ncause, being part of a community. Even when we don't love it, we don't want to be pushed out.\nBut liking it doesn't mean we're good at it, and a lack of communication with the people who\nhold the resources will cripple your ability to build the security you need. Some tips to make it\nrun smoother:\nDon't assume that because it makes sense to you, it makes sense to everyone. People don't\nlike change. Plan for that hurdle whenever you propose one, and show that the change is the\nnew normal. After all, the criminals targeting organizations like yours change their tools and\ntechniques constantly, and it's working for them.\nCommunicate according to your audience's capabilities. If they're tech-savvy, don't talk\ndown. If they aren't, don't bury them in tech-heavy reasoning.\nThink about the money before you talk. Your company exists to make money, so show value:\nhow added security can replace something you're already doing, or reduce downtime, add\nquality, or improve efficiency. (O4 helps you here: because it lets you put a number on your\nreachable surface, you can show risk going down as a measured fact, not a feeling.)\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 21\nDon't waste their time. Don't let them feel for a moment that you are. That means being\nprepared and polished.\nNever say \"I told you so.\" Don't bring up the time your suggestion got ignored and something\nbad happened. Don't act like you did them a favor or expect a reward for your brilliance. Start\nfrom zero each time.\nThe Big Picture\nIn cybersecurity you're surrounded by conflicting advice from authorities who are themselves\nstruggling to define what \"good\" security even is. The most basic questions leave professionals\nguessing: Is it more secure to harden servers or use a firewall? When do you need two-factor?\nCertificates or passwords? Do reverse proxies really add security?\nFor a long time those questions had no settled answer because the field had no instrument. No\nway to measure security, only ways to argue about it. So it fell back on measuring effort: how\nhard you tried, how many products you bought, how many alerts you watched. That was always\nthe wrong yardstick. The whole history of attacks is the history of effort-based security failing:\nthe big-number lock that was safe only because factoring it took too long, right up until the\nmath got faster and the \"hardness\" simply evaporated. Security that rests on how long\nsomething takes was always standing on sand.\nO4 is the field finally getting its instrument. It says, plainly, that security is not how hard you\nmake the attack but what shape you give your organization, and that shape can be measured.\nYour risk isn't a bet about a bad future; it's the distance, right now, from where you sit to your\nnearest failure. Shrink the reachable surface and that distance grows, measurably. Read intent\nat the boundary and you win in the only phase where winning is possible. Build the controls into\nthe structure, at zero distance, like a lock whose shape simply won't admit the wrong key, and\nyou stop paying the forever-tax of watching.\nThat reframes the humbling truth every security veteran knows. Things don't go bad gradually;\nthey go bad suddenly, from one breath to the next, the way you go from running like a gazelle to\nsliding across the ground with no idea why you fell. The reason it feels that way is that effort-\nbased security hides your real exposure until the moment it's spent. Measured security doesn't.\nYou can see the distance to the edge before you reach it.\nThe online security community still has more advice than you could ever use, and much of it\nconflicts, because leaning on the community gets you mostly the answers that let the\ncommunity, especially its vendors, profit. That skews the research, skews the tools, and skews\nwhat you think you need to know. Good security professionals tell you what you want to know;\ngreat ones tell you what you need to know, and you'll know it's the latter because it'll frustrate\nyou and make you wish you could just buy and install a product. You can buy the product. But\nwithout the work to fit it to your environment and your processes, you won't fix the problem.\nYou'll more likely create new ones.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 22\nSo treat the community as a resource, not an answer, the way Wikipedia is a resource, not a\nterm paper. What you get from it rarely fits as-is. You still have to answer your own riddle, and\nthat takes knowing your environment, your employees, and how it all works together: the Big\nPicture. Know where the interactions are, what resources are used and from where, and who\nhas authorized access to what. The difference now is that you no longer have to solve that\nriddle blind. O4 gives you the coordinate system, and this playbook gives you the moves: shrink\nwhat's reachable, read intent before contact, build the control into the shape. Do that and\nsecurity stops being a slogan you repeat and becomes something you can measure, hold, and\nprove, season after season, including against the attacks nobody's invented yet.\nAbout\nThe Institute for Security and Open Methodologies (ISECOM) is a non-profit, open research\norganization focused on all things related to security and hacking. ISECOM created and\nmaintains the Open Source Security Testing Methodology Manual (OSSTMM) and Hacker\nHighschool, designed to teach cybersecurity to teens.\nLearn more at www.isecom.org.\nA Short Glossary for This Edition\nAttack surface. How much of your organization is reachable right now: the sum of every path\nan attack could travel and every trust it could borrow. In O4, risk is the present-tense size of this\nsurface, not a probability about the future. You shrink it by removing paths and couplings you\ndon't need.\nRAV (Risk Assessment Value). The number OSSTMM 4 produces when you measure your\norganization against the full framework. It converts \"how reachable am I\" from an argument into\na score, computed from your controls, your coverage gaps, and your limitations. The field's\ninstrument. What makes \"we're more secure than we were last quarter\" either true or false in a\nway everyone can see.\nBuild it in, not bolt it on. Putting a control into the structure of a system, at zero distance to\nwhere the interaction happens, instead of adding an outside watcher to react after the fact. A\nlock that won't admit the wrong key's shape, versus a guard who notices the wrong key was\nused.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 23\nIntent / React / Resolve. The three phases of any interaction: before contact, during contact,\nafter contact. Defenders have real advantage in Intent and mostly forensics in React and\nResolve.\nIntent scanning. Reading what something is about to do before you let it act or enter: the\nreconnaissance aimed at your people and network, and the intent of software (its provenance,\nits behavior against known attacker techniques, and whether its risk changed since the last\nversion) read at the gate, before it runs.\nSecurity as geometry. The idea that strong security comes from shape, not effort: arranging a\nsystem so an unwanted interaction has no matching path to grab onto, so it can't happen rather\nthan merely being hard.\nThe reachable-surface rule (formerly \"the Somebody Sequence\"). The more a person is\nplaced inside a security process, the larger that process's reachable surface becomes. Take\nthe person out of the loop where you can.\nTrust as a coupling. A granted trust is a connection an attacker can borrow instead of break.\nPhishing, social engineering, and SQL injection all work by riding a trust rather than defeating a\nlock. Removing an unneeded trust removes the attack that would have used it.\nThe Open Source Cybersecurity Playbook O4 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 4.0 · © 2026 ISECOM 24\n", "isecom-jat-0": "# ISECOM JOAT (Jack of All Trades) — I S E C O M  P U B L I C A T I O N S\n\nI S E C O M  P U B L I C A T I O N S\nJack of\nAll Trades\nVERSION 3 / OCTOBER 2026\nISECOM\nInstitute for Security and\nOpen Methodologies\nVERSION HISTORY\n3 / October 2026\n2 / April 20, 2009\n1 / March 19, 2001\nAbout This Document\nJack of All Trades (JAT) is an instructor-led classroom exercise for teaching security thinking\nthrough non-security scenarios. It is not a test to hand out and grade. It is a guided discussion\nwhere the instructor uses everyday situations to draw out how students reason about protection,\naccess, trust, verification, and control.\nJAT is part of the OSSTMM (Open Source Security Testing Methodology Manual) training program\nand is used in OPST and OPSA certification courses. It works because security is not a technology\ndiscipline. It is a reasoning discipline. The same thinking that protects a building protects a\nnetwork. The same logic that verifies a patient's identity verifies a user's session. JAT makes that\ntransferable reasoning visible.\nThe instructor's role is not to grade answers as right or wrong. It is to use the answers as entry\npoints into deeper discussion: why did you think of that? What assumption were you making?\nWhat would break if that assumption was wrong?\nHow to Use This Document\nFormat: Instructor-led discussion. The instructor reads or paraphrases each scenario aloud and\nposes the questions. Students answer individually (written, timed) or in open discussion,\ndepending on class size and goals.\nTiming: 8 to 10 minutes per question when written. Open discussion naturally takes longer. A full\nsession of 4 scenarios runs approximately 90 minutes with debrief.\nSelection: You do not need to run all scenarios in one session. Choose scenarios that fit the class\nlevel and the security concepts you want to surface. The Instructor Notes under each scenario tell\nyou what it is designed to reveal, including which OSSTMM analysis concept it teaches best.\nDebrief: The debrief after each scenario is where learning happens. Do not skip it. Use the\nInstructor Notes to connect student answers back to security principles. Ask follow-up questions:\n\"What would change if...?\" \"What are you trusting when you do that?\" \"How would you verify that?\"\nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 2\nEvaluation: JAT does not produce a score. It produces a picture of how someone thinks about\nsecurity. Look for:\nBreadth vs depth. Does the student generate many surface-level ideas, or fewer ideas with\nreasoning behind them?\nAttacker thinking. Does the student naturally consider how their protections could be\ndefeated?\nTrust awareness. Does the student notice where they are making trust assumptions?\nOperational realism. Are the student's ideas things that could actually be implemented?\n• \n• \n• \n• \nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 3\nPart One: Foundations\nThese scenarios introduce security reasoning through physical and everyday situations. No\ntechnical background required. Suitable for all levels.\nScenario 1: Electrician\nYou are an electrician. In front of you is a light hanging from the ceiling. Behind you is a light\nswitch on the wall. The light is currently on.\nList 10 ways to turn off the light.\nList 10 components of a functioning light.\nList 10 ways to verify the light is actually off and not just appearing off.\nList 10 ways to prevent someone from being able to turn the light back on.\nEvidence Hierarchy (verification vs assumption)\nQ3 is the key question and it teaches the entire evidence hierarchy in miniature. Students\nwho only list visual checks (\"look at it\") are trusting one channel of observation. They are\ngenerating Level 1 evidence: self-reported, single-source. Push them: what if the bulb is\nbehind a wall? What if it is an indicator light and the circuit is still live?\nThis is the difference between observing a state and verifying a state. In OSSTMM terms, it\nis the difference between evidence gathered by asking (the light looks off) and evidence\ngathered by testing (I measured zero voltage across the terminals).\nQ1 and Q2 are enumeration exercises. Q1 enumerates the interaction points where you can\naffect the system. Q2 enumerates the components, which is attack surface mapping. Q4\nmaps to specific security controls: removing the switch is reducing attack surface, locking\nthe switch is access control, cutting the wire is eliminating the channel entirely.\n\"You just built an evidence hierarchy without knowing it. Your weakest answers to Q3 were 'look\nat it.' Your strongest were 'test the circuit with a meter.' In security testing, this same range\nexists: you can ask someone if their firewall works, or you can test the firewall yourself. The\nmethodology cares about which one you did.\"\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4. \nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 4\nScenario 2: Delivery Driver\nYou are a driver for a logistics company. You have a package to deliver to a residential address.\nYou have never been to this address before.\nList 10 ways to confirm you have the right recipient.\nList 10 things that could prevent you from completing the delivery.\nList 10 reasons a package should be delivered at all rather than returned to sender.\nList 10 ways to verify the identity of the sender.\nTrust Properties\nQ1 is identity verification. Students who list \"ask their name\" are using single-factor\nauthentication. Those who combine name, ID, address, and expectation of delivery are\nlayering trust verification across multiple properties: consistency, completeness, and\ntransparency.\nThe delivery driver is a trust proxy. The sender trusts the driver to deliver correctly. The\nrecipient trusts the driver to have a legitimate package. But neither trusts the other\ndirectly. The driver mediates trust between parties with no direct relationship.\nQ4 flips the direction: verifying the source, not the destination. Students who breeze\nthrough Q1 but struggle with Q4 are revealing a common blind spot. Upstream trust\nmatters as much as downstream authentication.\n\"Trust in this scenario has properties you can name and test. Is the trust between sender and\ndriver symmetric? Is it transparent? What happens when one property fails but others hold?\nThese are measurable qualities of a trust relationship, not just a feeling.\"\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4. \nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 5\nScenario 3: Landlord\nYou own a small apartment building with six units. A new tenant is moving in.\nList 10 things you need to verify about the new tenant before giving them keys.\nList 10 ways the tenant could compromise the security of the building for other tenants.\nList 10 responsibilities you have for maintaining the security of the building.\nList 10 ways a former tenant who still has a key could cause problems.\nTrust Lifecycle (provisioning through revocation)\nThe arc from Q1 to Q4 IS the trust lifecycle. Q1 is trust establishment. Q3 is trust\nmaintenance. Q4 is trust revocation failure: what happens when access persists after the\ntrust relationship ends.\nThe gap between provisioning and revocation is where most access-control failures\nactually occur. Organizations are generally better at granting access than revoking it.\nTrust/Privacy Inverse (strong fit): Q1 is the clearest example. Verifying a new tenant\nrequires accessing their financial history, employment records, references, and identity\ndocuments. The tenant must sacrifice privacy to establish trust. This is the fundamental\nmechanism by which trust operates.\n\"You just mapped a trust lifecycle: establish, maintain, revoke. Most security programs invest\nheavily in step one and barely think about step three. The landlord who never changes the locks\nafter a tenant leaves has the same problem as the IT department that never disables accounts\nafter an employee departs.\"\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4. \nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 6\nScenario 4: Doctor\nYou are a doctor in a walk-in clinic. A parent brings in a child who is unresponsive.\nList 10 things you need to assess before beginning treatment.\nList 10 ways to determine what happened to the child without the parent's account.\nList 10 precautions you must take to protect yourself and the child during examination.\nList 10 ways to verify the parent's account of what happened is accurate.\nIndependent Evidence vs Self-Attestation\nQ2 is the heart of this scenario. The parent's account is self-attestation. It may be\naccurate, incomplete, mistaken, or deliberately false. The doctor cannot know which\nwithout independent evidence: physical examination, toxicology, medical history,\nwitnesses, injury patterns.\nThis maps directly to security testing. When a vendor says their product blocks attacks,\nthat is self-attestation. When an internal team says they patched the vulnerability, that is\nself-attestation. Only independent testing produces verified evidence.\nQ4 extends this: the person reporting the incident and the person responsible may be the\nsame person. In security, this is the insider threat.\n\"The doctor who treats based only on what the parent says is the security team that accepts\nthe vendor's compliance certificate without testing. Q2 asked you to find evidence that does\nnot come from the interested party. That distinction is the single most important concept in\nsecurity assessment.\"\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4. \nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 7\nPart Two: Operations\nThese scenarios introduce more complex reasoning about systems, processes, and\nadversarial thinking. Suitable for mid-level and above.\nScenario 5: Soldier\nYou are a soldier stationed at the only bridge crossing a river gorge. You expect hostile forces\nfrom the other side.\nList 10 ways to prepare your position before the enemy arrives.\nList 10 ways to prevent the enemy from crossing the bridge.\nList 10 ways to distinguish friendly civilians from enemy combatants.\nList 10 consequences of destroying the bridge entirely.\nControls Taxonomy\nQ2 is the best teaching question. Each answer maps to a specific type of security control.\n\"Block the bridge with barriers\" is Channeling. \"Post sentries\" is Surveillance. \"Require\nidentification\" is Authentication. \"Limit crossing to certain hours\" is Throttling.\nQ3 is authentication under adversarial conditions. The enemy is actively trying to appear\nfriendly. This is social engineering and impersonation.\nQ4 is the trade-off question. Destroying the bridge eliminates the threat channel\ncompletely but also eliminates your own access, supply lines, and future flexibility. Every\nfirewall rule that blocks a port also blocks legitimate traffic on that port.\n\"Each of your answers to Q2 was a specific type of control. You intuitively sorted them into\nsurveillance, authentication, access restriction, and channel management. The methodology\nnames these categories because a defense built from only one type of control has a\npredictable weakness.\"\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4. \nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 8\nScenario 6: Safety Inspector\nYou are a licensed safety inspector called to a factory after a series of workplace accidents.\nList 10 questions you would ask the factory foreman in your first hour.\nList 10 things you would physically inspect without asking anyone.\nList 10 changes that would make the factory safer that the workers would resist.\nList 10 ways management could make the accident record look better without actually\nimproving safety.\nEvidence Hierarchy (self-report vs independent testing)\nQ1 and Q2 teach the evidence hierarchy through direct contrast. Q1 gathers evidence by\nasking. Q2 gathers evidence by looking. The gap between these two sets of answers is the\ngap between compliance and actual security.\nQ3 introduces the human element: security controls that users resist get bypassed.\nShadow IT, password workarounds, propping open fire doors. A control that works\nperfectly but that people route around is not a working control.\nQ4 is explicitly about gaming metrics. Reclassify accidents. Underreport near-misses.\nChange definitions. Each makes the safety record improve on paper while the factory\nremains exactly as dangerous.\n\"Q1 and Q2 were the same investigation using two different evidence sources. Everything you\nlearned from Q1 came from the foreman. Everything from Q2 came from your own observation.\nWhen those disagree, which one do you trust? That question is the entire argument for\nevidence-based security testing.\"\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4. \nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 9\nScenario 7: Building Inspector\nYou are a bridge and building inspector for a major city. You must assess an aging bridge for\nsafety.\nList 10 structural areas you would inspect.\nList 10 concerns the city would have about shutting the bridge for inspection.\nList 10 ways the bridge could fail that would not be visible from a surface inspection.\nList 10 things you would need to verify about the last inspection report before trusting it.\nEvidence Decay\nQ4 introduces the principle that evidence has a shelf life. A previous inspection report is\nlike a previous penetration test report. Before trusting it: How old is it? Who performed it?\nWhat methodology? What was in scope? What has changed since?\nA report that says \"safe\" is a statement about a specific moment in time. Every day after,\nthe evidence decays. The environment changes and the evidence has not been refreshed.\nSecurity evidence works the same way.\nQ3 reinforces from a different angle: hidden failure modes. Internal corrosion, fatigue\ncracks in load-bearing members. These map to vulnerabilities that do not show up in\nexternal scans or compliance reviews. Surface inspection generates surface evidence.\n\"Every answer you gave for Q4 was a reason the previous report might no longer be true. That is\nevidence decay. The report was accurate when it was written. Time made it less accurate. A\npenetration test is not a certificate. It is a snapshot.\"\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4. \nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 10\nScenario 8: Mechanic\nYou are a mechanic at an automotive shop. A customer brings in a used car they are considering\npurchasing and wants your professional assessment.\nList 10 questions you would ask the seller about the car.\nList 10 things you would inspect that the seller is unlikely to mention.\nList 10 signs that the car's history has been misrepresented.\nList 10 reasons the buyer should get a second independent inspection.\nAssessment Independence\nQ4 is the payoff: why does the buyer need a second opinion? Because every assessment\ncarries the assessor's biases, blind spots, methodology, and relationship to the parties. A\nsingle mechanic might miss something, have a financial incentive, or be competent in one\narea but weak in another.\nSeparation of tester from implementer is not a procedural formality. It is an evidence-\nquality requirement.\nQ1 vs Q2 reinforces the evidence hierarchy. Q1 is what the seller volunteers. Q2 is what you\nfind independently. Q3 is detecting deception in self-reported information: the automotive\nequivalents of falsified compliance evidence.\n\"Q4 asked why one assessment is not enough. Every reason you listed was a form of the same\nprinciple: no single assessor can guarantee completeness, and any assessor with a relationship\nto the outcome has a potential bias. Independence in assessment is not distrust. It is\nmethodology.\"\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4. \nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 11\nPart Three: Adversarial Thinking\nThese scenarios ask students to think as an attacker, a manipulator, or an exploiter. Suitable\nfor advanced students and practitioners.\nScenario 9: Thief\nYou are a professional diamond thief who works alone at night.\nList 10 ways to choose the best target.\nList 10 security measures you would expect to encounter.\nList 10 things you would do to avoid detection during the job.\nList 10 ways to increase the value of what you take from each job.\nFive-Point Process (5PP) from the attacker's perspective\nThe attacker's workflow IS the tester's workflow viewed from the other side. Q1 is target\nselection and scoping. Q2 is reconnaissance and enumeration. Q3 is operational execution\nwith detection avoidance. Q4 goes beyond the initial operation into post-exploitation.\nQ1: Students who list \"most valuable inventory\" think about asset value. Those who list\n\"weakest security\" think about vulnerability. Those who list \"best ratio of value to risk\"\nthink about operational efficiency. The best answers combine all three.\n\"You just walked through a security test from the attacker's side. Q1 was scoping. Q2 was\nenumeration. Q3 was execution. Q4 was exploitation. The methodology formalizes this same\nworkflow, because understanding how an attacker operates is how you learn to test defenses.\"\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4. \nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 12\nScenario 10: Con Artist\nYou need to gain access to a private event at a hotel where you are not on the guest list. You have\nno invitation, no badge, and no contact inside.\nList 10 ways to get into the event.\nList 10 things you would observe from outside before attempting entry.\nList 10 things that could go wrong during your attempt.\nList 10 ways the event organizer could have prevented your entry.\nFive-Point Process (reconnaissance) and Trust/Privacy Inverse\nQ2 is pure reconnaissance and the strongest 5PP teaching moment. The best attackers\nspend more time observing than acting. Staff rotation patterns, badge checking\nconsistency, service entrance traffic, which doors are propped open.\nEach observation invades the event's operational privacy. The event's security relies partly\non its procedures not being visible to outsiders. The more the con artist observes, the\nweaker the defenses become.\nQ4 flips to defense: the round-trip from attack to defend is the core of security testing.\nBut it also surfaces the defender's dilemma: preventing the con artist requires being less\nopen, more restrictive. The defenses that block the attacker also make the event less\nwelcoming for legitimate guests.\n\"Q2 was reconnaissance. Everything you learned by watching made your attack more likely to\nsucceed. The event's security depended partly on you NOT having that information. That\nrelationship, where your increased knowledge reduces their security, is built into how trust and\nprivacy interact.\"\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4. \nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 13\nScenario 11: Smuggler\nYou need to move a small, valuable object across a national border. The object is legal to possess\nbut the border authority will confiscate it if they find it.\nList 10 ways to conceal the object during crossing.\nList 10 things border security will check during inspection.\nList 10 ways to cross without being inspected at all.\nList 10 ways a border authority could detect your concealment after the fact.\nBypass vs Evasion\nQ3 is the most important question. Q1 asks how to defeat the control (evasion). Q3 asks\nhow to avoid the control entirely (bypass). These are fundamentally different operations.\nEvasion means you interact with the control and attempt to pass through undetected.\nBypass means you find a path where the control does not exist. In almost every real-world\nscenario, bypass is easier, because it means finding a gap in coverage rather than\ndefeating detection capability.\nIn security terms: Q1 is evading a firewall rule. Q3 is finding an unmonitored path that does\nnot pass through the firewall at all.\nQ4 introduces forensic thinking. Even if the smuggler succeeds, can the defender\nreconstruct what happened after the fact? A control that does not prevent an action but\ncreates evidence of it is still a control.\n\"Q1 and Q3 taught you the difference between evasion and bypass. Evasion defeats a control.\nBypass avoids it. When testing security, finding a bypass is usually more significant, because it\nmeans the control had a coverage gap.\"\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4. \nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 14\nPart Four: Systems Thinking\nThese scenarios involve complex environments with multiple stakeholders, trade-offs, and\ncompeting priorities. Suitable for senior practitioners and leadership.\nScenario 12: School Principal\nYou are the principal of a public school. A series of thefts has occurred from students' lockers.\nList 10 possible causes of the thefts beyond \"someone is stealing.\"\nList 10 measures you could take that would reduce theft without making the school feel like a\nprison.\nList 10 ways your anti-theft measures could be misused or create new problems.\nList 10 things you would need to explain to parents about why zero theft is not a realistic\ngoal.\nRAV (proportional measurement of security)\nQ1 forces root-cause thinking. In security, the presenting incident is often not the real\nproblem. Measurement starts with correct diagnosis.\nQ2 is about proportional controls. The principal could install cameras everywhere and\nsearch every backpack. That would reduce theft and destroy the learning environment.\nThe measure of a control is not just its effectiveness but its cost relative to what it\nprotects. This is the RAV concept: security is a ratio, not an absolute.\nQ3 teaches that controls are attack surface too. Cameras become surveillance tools.\nMonitoring creates data that can be leaked or misused. Every control introduces new risks.\nQ4 is communicating risk to non-technical stakeholders. Absolute security does not exist.\nThe ability to explain that honestly is a leadership skill every CISO needs.\n\"Q2 was about proportional controls. Q3 was about the cost of those controls. Q4 was about\ncommunicating what is realistically achievable. Security measurement accounts for all three.\"\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4. \nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 15\nScenario 13: Event Planner\nYou are organizing a public outdoor concert for 5,000 people.\nList 10 things that could go wrong that are not related to the performers or the music.\nList 10 ways you would control who enters and exits the venue.\nList 10 pieces of information you would need about each vendor working the event.\nList 10 things you would do differently if you learned a credible threat had been made against\nthe event.\nControls (Channeling) and Third-Party Trust\nQ2 is Channeling at scale. With 5,000 people, you cannot individually authenticate each\none. You must create controlled flow: entry points, exit points, perimeter barriers,\ncredential checks at chokepoints. The number of entry points and their verification\nrequirements determine both security and usability.\nQ3 is third-party trust assessment. Vendors are your supply chain. They have physical\naccess. What do you need to know about each one?\nQ4 introduces threat-informed posture adjustment. Students who add evacuation\nplanning, communication protocols, and law enforcement coordination are thinking about\nresponse and resilience, not just prevention.\n\"Q2 was about Channeling. Q3 was about trusting your supply chain. Q4 was about adjusting\nposture when the threat changes. The Channeling decisions from Q2 constrain your response\noptions in Q4, and the vendor trust from Q3 determines whether your supply chain is part of\nyour defense or part of your exposure.\"\nFacilitator Reference: Mapping to OSSTMM\nConcepts\nEach scenario exercises specific OSSTMM analysis concepts. Use this table to select scenarios\nbased on what you want to teach.\n1. \n2. \n3. \n4. \nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 16\nOSSTMM Concept Primary Scenario(s) Supporting Scenario(s)\nEvidence Hierarchy 1 (Electrician), 6 (Safety\nInspector)\n4 (Doctor), 8 (Mechanic)\nTrust Properties 2 (Delivery Driver) 3 (Landlord), 10 (Con Artist)\nTrust Lifecycle 3 (Landlord) 2 (Delivery Driver)\nTrust/Privacy Inverse 3 (Landlord), 4 (Doctor), 10\n(Con Artist)\n2 (Delivery Driver), 8 (Mechanic)\nControls Taxonomy 5 (Soldier) 1 (Electrician), 13 (Event\nPlanner)\nFive-Point Process (5PP) 9 (Thief), 10 (Con Artist) 6 (Safety Inspector), 7 (Building\nInspector)\nEvidence Decay 7 (Building Inspector)\nAssessment Independence 8 (Mechanic) 4 (Doctor)\nBypass vs Evasion 11 (Smuggler)\nRAV / Proportional\nMeasurement\n12 (School Principal) 5 (Soldier)\nMetrics Gaming / False\nAssurance\n6 (Safety Inspector) 7 (Building Inspector)\nRisk Communication 12 (School Principal) 13 (Event Planner)\nChanneling 13 (Event Planner) 5 (Soldier)\nThird-Party / Supply Chain\nTrust\n13 (Event Planner) 8 (Mechanic)\nSecond-Order Effects of\nControls\n12 (School Principal) 6 (Safety Inspector)\nCopyleft Notice\nThis work is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivs 3.0 Unported\nLicense (CC BY-NC-ND 3.0) and the Open Methodology License (OML).\nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 17\nYou are free to share, copy, and redistribute this material in any medium or format under the following terms:\nyou must give appropriate credit to ISECOM, you may not use the material for commercial purposes, and you\nmay not distribute modified versions of this material.\nThe full text of the CC BY-NC-ND 3.0 license is available at https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-\nnd/3.0/ and the Open Methodology License at https://www.isecom.org/oml.html\n© 2000–2026 ISECOM — Institute for Security and Open Methodologies. All rights reserved.\nResearch and development maintained by ISECOM. Contact www.isecom.org for licensing questions.\nJack of All Trades v.3 ISECOM\nCC BY-NC-ND 3.0 + OML 18\n"};
