239": "# S1239 TONESHELL\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1239\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1239\n\n[TONESHELL](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1239) is a custom backdoor that has been used since at least Q1 2021.(Citation: Palo Alto Unit42 STATELY TAURUS TONESHELL September 2023)   [TONESHELL](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1239) malware has previously been leveraged by Chinese affiliated actors identified as [Mustang Panda](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0129).(Citation: ATTACKIQ MUSTANG PANDA TONESHELL March 2023)(Citation: Zscaler)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1480.001 Environmental Keying\n- T1480 Execution Guardrails\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1678 Delay Execution\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1497.002 User Activity Based Checks\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1027.007 Dynamic API Resolution\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1132.002 Non-Standard Encoding\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1010 Application Window Discovery\n- T1218.010 Regsvr32\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n- T1055.001 Dynamic-link Library Injection\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1027.001 Binary Padding\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1622 Debugger Evasion\n- T1218.013 Mavinject\n- T1134.002 Create Process with Token\n- T1087 Account Discovery\n- T1480.002 Mutual Exclusion\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1205 Traffic Signaling\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1027.012 LNK Icon Smuggling\n- T1559 Inter-Process Communication\n- T1001.003 Protocol or Service Impersonation\n- T1560.001 Archive via Utility\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1164": "# S1164 UPSTYLE\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1164\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1164\n\n[UPSTYLE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1164) is a Python-based backdoor associated with exploitation of Palo Alto firewalls using CVE-2024-3400 in early 2024. [UPSTYLE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1164) has only been observed in relation to this exploitation activity, which involved attempted install on compromised devices by the threat actor UTA0218.(Citation: Volexity UPSTYLE 2024)(Citation: Palo Alto MidnightEclipse APR 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1685.006 Clear Linux or Mac System Logs\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1036 Masquerading\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1001.001 Junk Data\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n- T1102.003 One-Way Communication\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1546 Event Triggered Execution\n- T1059.006 Python\n- T1665 Hide Infrastructure\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0088": "# S0088 Kasidet\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0088\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0088\n\n[Kasidet](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0088) is a backdoor that has been dropped by using malicious VBA macros. (Citation: Zscaler Kasidet)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1686 Disable or Modify System Firewall\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1211": "# S1211 Hannotog\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1211\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1211\n\n[Hannotog](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1211) is a type of backdoor malware uniquely assoicated with [Lotus Blossom](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0030) operations since at least 2022.(Citation: Symantec Bilbug 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1571 Non-Standard Port\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1020 Automated Exfiltration\n- T1686 Disable or Modify System Firewall\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0346": "# S0346 OceanSalt\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0346\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0346\n\n[OceanSalt](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0346) is a Trojan that was used in a campaign targeting victims in South Korea, United States, and Canada. [OceanSalt](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0346) shares code similarity with [SpyNote RAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0305), which has been linked to [APT1](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0006).(Citation: McAfee Oceansalt Oct 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1132.002 Non-Standard Encoding\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1162": "# S1162 Playcrypt\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1162\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1162\n\n[Playcrypt](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1162) is a ransomware that has been used by [Play](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1040) since at least 2022 in attacks against against the business, government, critical infrastructure, healthcare, and media sectors in North America, South America, and Europe. [Playcrypt](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1162) derives its name from adding the .play extension to encrypted files and has overlap with tactics and tools associated with Hive and Nokoyawa ransomware and infrastructure associated with Quantum ransomware.(Citation: Microsoft PlayCrypt August 2022)(Citation: CISA Play Ransomware Advisory December 2023)(Citation: Trend Micro Ransomware Spotlight Play July 2023)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0252": "# S0252 Brave Prince\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0252\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0252\n\n[Brave Prince](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0252) is a Korean-language implant that was first observed in the wild in December 2017. It contains similar code and behavior to [Gold Dragon](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0249), and was seen along with [Gold Dragon](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0249) and [RunningRAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0253) in operations surrounding the 2018 Pyeongchang Winter Olympics. (Citation: McAfee Gold Dragon)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1048.003 Exfiltration Over Unencrypted Non-C2 Protocol\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1244": "# S1244 Medusa Ransomware\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1244\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1244\n\n[Medusa Ransomware](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1244) has been utilized in attacks since at least 2021. [Medusa Ransomware](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1244) has been known to be utilized in conjunction with living off the land techniques and remote management software. [Medusa Ransomware](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1244) has been used in campaigns associated with “double extortion” ransomware activity, where data is exfiltrated from victim environments prior to encryption, with threats to publish files if a ransom is not paid. [Medusa Ransomware](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1244) software was initially a closed ransomware variant which later evolved to a Ransomware as a Service (RaaS). [Medusa Ransomware](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1244) has impacted victims from a diverse range of sectors within a multitude of countries, and it is assessed [Medusa Ransomware](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1244) is used in an opportunistic manner.(Citation: CISA Medusa Group Medusa Ransomware March 2025)(Citation: Security Scorecard Medusa Ransomware January 2024)(Citation: Palo Alto Unit 42 Medusa Group Medusa Ransomware January 2024)(Citation: Broadcom Medusa Ransomware Medusa Group March 2025)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1679 Selective Exclusion\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1564.003 Hidden Window\n- T1559 Inter-Process Communication\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0629": "# S0629 RainyDay\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0629\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0629\n\n[RainyDay](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0629) is a backdoor tool that has been used by [Naikon](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0019) since at least 2020.(Citation: Bitdefender Naikon April 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1567.002 Exfiltration to Cloud Storage\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode