very\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1217 Browser Information Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0612": "# S0612 WastedLocker\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0612\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0612\n\n[WastedLocker](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0612) is a ransomware family attributed to [Indrik Spider](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0119) that has been used since at least May 2020. [WastedLocker](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0612) has been used against a broad variety of sectors, including manufacturing, information technology, and media.(Citation: Symantec WastedLocker June 2020)(Citation: NCC Group WastedLocker June 2020)(Citation: Sentinel Labs WastedLocker July 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1027.016 Junk Code Insertion\n- T1120 Peripheral Device Discovery\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1564.004 NTFS File Attributes\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1222.001 Windows Permissions\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0511": "# S0511 RegDuke\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0511\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0511\n\n[RegDuke](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0511) is a first stage implant written in .NET and used by [APT29](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0016) since at least 2017. [RegDuke](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0511) has been used to control a compromised machine when control of other implants on the machine was lost.(Citation: ESET Dukes October 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1027.003 Steganography\n- T1546.003 Windows Management Instrumentation Event Subscription\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1027.011 Fileless Storage\n- T1102.002 Bidirectional Communication\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0654": "# S0654 ProLock\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0654\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0654\n\n[ProLock](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0654) is a ransomware strain that has been used in Big Game Hunting (BGH) operations since at least 2020, often obtaining initial access with [QakBot](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0650). [ProLock](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0654) is the successor to PwndLocker ransomware which was found to contain a bug allowing decryption without ransom payment in 2019.(Citation: Group IB Ransomware September 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1197 BITS Jobs\n- T1027.003 Steganography\n- T1068 Exploitation for Privilege Escalation\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1137": "# S1137 Moneybird\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1137\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1137\n\n[Moneybird](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1137) is a ransomware variant written in C++ associated with [Agrius](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1030) operations. The name \"Moneybird\" is contained in the malware's ransom note and as strings in the executable.(Citation: CheckPoint Agrius 2023)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1027.009 Embedded Payloads\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0260": "# S0260 InvisiMole\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0260\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0260\n\n[InvisiMole](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0260) is a modular spyware program that has been used by the InvisiMole Group since at least 2013. [InvisiMole](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0260) has two backdoor modules called RC2FM and RC2CL that are used to perform post-exploitation activities. It has been discovered on compromised victims in the Ukraine and Russia. [Gamaredon Group](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0047) infrastructure has been used to download and execute [InvisiMole](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0260) against a small number of victims.(Citation: ESET InvisiMole June 2018)(Citation: ESET InvisiMole June 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1686 Disable or Modify System Firewall\n- T1025 Data from Removable Media\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1055.002 Portable Executable Injection\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1008 Fallback Channels\n- T1559.001 Component Object Model\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1055.004 Asynchronous Procedure Call\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1560.002 Archive via Library\n- T1560.001 Archive via Utility\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1010 Application Window Discovery\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1068 Exploitation for Privilege Escalation\n- T1132.002 Non-Standard Encoding\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1218.002 Control Panel\n- T1059.007 JavaScript\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1480.001 Environmental Keying\n- T1055.015 ListPlanting\n- T1090.001 Internal Proxy\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1123 Audio Capture\n- T1210 Exploitation of Remote Services\n- T1087.001 Local Account\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1560.003 Archive via Custom Method\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1001.003 Protocol or Service Impersonation\n- T1070.005 Network Share Connection Removal\n- T1119 Automated Collection\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1564.003 Hidden Window\n- T1080 Taint Shared Content\n- T1125 Video Capture\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1547.009 Shortcut Modification\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1046 Network Service Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1236": "# S1236 CLAIMLOADER\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1236\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1236\n\n[CLAIMLOADER](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1236) is a malware variant that frequently accompanies legitimate executables that are used for DLL side-loading known to be leveraged by [Mustang Panda](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0129) and was first observed utilized in 2021.(Citation: IBM MUSTANG PANDA PUBLOAD CLAIMLOADER JUNE 2025)(Citation: 2025_IBM_PUBLOAD_TONESHELL_HIUPAN_CLAIMLOADER_MUSTANG PANDA)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1559.001 Component Object Model\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1480.002 Mutual Exclusion\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1027.007 Dynamic API Resolution\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0598": "# S0598 P.A.S. Webshell\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0598\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0598\n\n[P.A.S. Webshell](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0598) is a publicly available multifunctional PHP webshell in use since at least 2016 that provides remote access and execution on target web servers.(Citation: ANSSI Sandworm January 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1505.003 Web Shell\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1222.002 Linux and Mac Permissions\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1059 Command and Scripting Interpreter\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1046 Network Service Discovery\n- T1213.006 Databases\n- T1110.001 Password Guessing\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1087.001 Local Account\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1084": "# S1084 QUIETEXIT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1084\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1084\n\n[QUIETEXIT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1084) is a novel backdoor, based on the open-source Dropbear SSH client-server software, that has been used by [APT29](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0016) since at least 2021. [APT29](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0016) has deployed [QUIETEXIT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1084) on opaque network appliances that typically don't support antivirus or endpoint detection and response tools within a victim environment.(Citation: Mandiant APT29 Eye Spy Email Nov 22)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1090.002 External Proxy\n- T1008 Fallback Channels\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1071 Application Layer Protocol\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0205": "# S0205 Naid\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0205\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0205\n\n[Naid](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0205) is a trojan used by [Elderwood](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0066) to open a backdoor on compromised hosts. (Citation: Symantec Elderwood Sept 2012) (Citation: Symantec Naid June 2012)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1133": "# S1133 Apostle\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1133\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1133\n\n[Apostle](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1133) is malware that has functioned as both a wiper and, in more recent versions, as ransomware.  [Apostle](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1133) is written in .NET and shares various programming and functional overlaps with [IPsec Helper](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1132).(Citation: SentinelOne Agrius 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1685.005 Clear Windows Event Logs\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1529 System Shutdown/Reboot\n- T1480 Execution Guardrails\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1561.001 Disk Content Wipe\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1485 Data Destruction\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0180": "# S0180 Volgmer\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0180\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0180\n\n[Volgmer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0180) is a backdoor Trojan designed to provide covert access to a compromised system. It has been used since at least 2013 to target the government, financial, automotive, and media industries. Its primary delivery mechanism is suspected to be spearphishing. (Citation: US-CERT Volgmer Nov 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1027.011 Fileless Storage\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0219": "# S0219 WINERACK\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0219\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0219\n\n[WINERACK](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0219) is a backdoor used by [APT37](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0067). (Citation: FireEye APT37 Feb 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1010 Application Window Discovery\n- T1059 Command and Scripting Interpreter\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0689": "# S0689 WhisperGate\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0689\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0689\n\n[WhisperGate](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0689) is a multi-stage wiper designed to look like ransomware that has been used against multiple government, non-profit, and information technology organizations in Ukraine since at least January 2022.(Citation: Cybereason WhisperGate February 2022)(Citation: Unit 42 WhisperGate January 2022)(Citation: Microsoft WhisperGate January 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1542.003 Bootkit\n- T1620 Reflective Code Loading\n- T1485 Data Destruction\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1218.004 InstallUtil\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1561.002 Disk Structure Wipe\n- T1561.001 Disk Content Wipe\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1529 System Shutdown/Reboot\n- T1055.012 Process Hollowing\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1134.002 Create Process with Token\n- T1102 Web Service\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1497.003 Time Based Checks\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1036 Masquerading\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0277": "# S0277 FruitFly\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0277\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0277\n\nFruitFly is designed to spy on mac users  (Citation: objsee mac malware 2017).\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1027.010 Command Obfuscation\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1543.001 Launch Agent\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0230": "# S0230 ZeroT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0230\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0230\n\n[ZeroT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0230) is a Trojan used by [TA459](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0062), often in conjunction with [PlugX](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0013). (Citation: Proofpoint TA459 April 2017) (Citation: Proofpoint ZeroT Feb 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1027.016 Junk Code Insertion\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1001.002 Steganography\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0276": "# S0276 Keydnap\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0276\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0276\n\nThis piece of malware steals the content of the user's keychain while maintaining a permanent backdoor  (Citation: OSX Keydnap malware).\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1090.003 Multi-hop Proxy\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1555.002 Securityd Memory\n- T1564.009 Resource Forking\n- T1548.001 Setuid and Setgid\n- T1056.002 GUI Input Capture\n- T1036.006 Space after Filename\n- T1059.006 Python\n- T1543.001 Launch Agent\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1167": "# S1167 AcidPour\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1167\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1167\n\n[AcidPour](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1167) is a variant of [AcidRain](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1125) designed to impact a wider range of x86 architecture Linux devices. [AcidPour](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1167) is an x86 ELF binary that expands on the targeted devices and locations in [AcidRain](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1125) by including items such as Unsorted Block Image (UBI), Deice Mapper (DM), and various flash memory references. Based on this expanded targeting, [AcidPour](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1167) can impact a variety of device types including IoT, networking, and ICS embedded device types.(Citation: SentinelOne AcidPour 2024) [AcidPour](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1167) is a wiping payload associated with the [Sandworm Team](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0034) threat actor, and potentially linked to attacks against Ukrainian internet service providers (ISPs) in 2023.(Citation: CERT-UA TelecomAttack 2023)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1529 System Shutdown/Reboot\n- T1120 Peripheral Device Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1561.001 Disk Content Wipe\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1485 Data Destruction\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0495": "# S0495 RDAT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0495\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0495\n\n[RDAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0495) is a backdoor used by the suspected Iranian threat group [OilRig](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0049). [RDAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0495) was originally identified in 2017 and targeted companies in the telecommunications sector.(Citation: Unit42 RDAT July 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1027.003 Steganography\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1132.002 Non-Standard Encoding\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1071.003 Mail Protocols\n- T1001.002 Steganography\n- T1030 Data Transfer Size Limits\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1008 Fallback Channels\n- T1001 Data Obfuscation\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0047": "# S0047 Hacking Team UEFI Rootkit\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0047\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0047\n\n[Hacking Team UEFI Rootkit](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0047) is a rootkit developed by the company Hacking Team as a method of persistence for remote access software. (Citation: TrendMicro Hacking Team UEFI)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1014 Rootkit\n- T1542.001 System Firmware\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0468": "# S0468 Skidmap\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0468\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0468\n\n[Skidmap](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0468) is a kernel-mode rootkit used for cryptocurrency mining.(Citation: Trend Micro Skidmap)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1496.001 Compute Hijacking\n- T1098.004 SSH Authorized Keys\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1053.003 Cron\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1547.006 Kernel Modules and Extensions\n- T1556.003 Pluggable Authentication Modules\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1014 Rootkit\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0439": "# S0439 Okrum\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0439\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0439\n\n[Okrum](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0439) is a Windows backdoor that has been seen in use since December 2016 with strong links to [Ke3chang](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0004).(Citation: ESET Okrum July 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1003.005 Cached Domain Credentials\n- T1547.009 Shortcut Modification\n- T1560.001 Archive via Utility\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1497.002 User Activity Based Checks\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1497.003 Time Based Checks\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1001 Data Obfuscation\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1027.003 Steganography\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1134.001 Token Impersonation/Theft\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1090.002 External Proxy\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1560.003 Archive via Custom Method\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1003.001 LSASS Memory\n- T1001.003 Protocol or Service Impersonation\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1201": "# S1201 TRANSLATEXT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1201\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1201\n\n[TRANSLATEXT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1201) is malware that is believed to be used by [Kimsuky](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0094).(Citation: Zscaler Kimsuky TRANSLATEXT) [TRANSLATEXT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1201) masqueraded as a Google Translate extension for Google Chrome, but is actually a collection of four malicious Javascript files that perform defense evasion, information collection and exfiltration.(Citation: Zscaler Kimsuky TRANSLATEXT)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1185 Browser Session Hijacking\n- T1102.001 Dead Drop Resolver\n- T1539 Steal Web Session Cookie\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1176.001 Browser Extensions\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1114 Email Collection\n- T1102.002 Bidirectional Communication\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1205 Traffic Signaling\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0019": "# S0019 Regin\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0019\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0019\n\n[Regin](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0019) is a malware platform that has targeted victims in a range of industries, including telecom, government, and financial institutions. Some [Regin](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0019) timestamps date back to 2003. (Citation: Kaspersky Regin)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1564.004 NTFS File Attributes\n- T1564.005 Hidden File System\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1036.001 Invalid Code Signature\n- T1090.002 External Proxy\n- T1071.002 File Transfer Protocols\n- T1021.002 SMB/Windows Admin Shares\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1040 Network Sniffing\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0486": "# S0486 Bonadan\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0486\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0486\n\n[Bonadan](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0486) is a malicious version of OpenSSH which acts as a custom backdoor. [Bonadan](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0486) has been active since at least 2018 and combines a new cryptocurrency-mining module with the same credential-stealing module used by the Onderon family of backdoors.(Citation: ESET ForSSHe December 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1496.001 Compute Hijacking\n- T1554 Compromise Host Software Binary\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1059 Command and Scripting Interpreter\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1186": "# S1186 Line Dancer\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1186\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1186\n\n[Line Dancer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1186) is a memory-only Lua-based shellcode loader associated with the [ArcaneDoor](https://attack.mitre.org/campaigns/C0046) campaign. [Line Dancer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1186) allows an adversary to upload and execute arbitrary shellcode on victim devices.(Citation: Cisco ArcaneDoor 2024)(Citation: CCCS ArcaneDoor 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1690 Prevent Command History Logging\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1014 Rootkit\n- T1653 Power Settings\n- T1040 Network Sniffing\n- T1059.008 Network Device CLI\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0370": "# S0370 SamSam\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0370\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0370\n\n[SamSam](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0370) is ransomware that appeared in early 2016. Unlike some ransomware, its variants have required operators to manually interact with the malware to execute some of its core components.(Citation: US-CERT SamSam 2018)(Citation: Talos SamSam Jan 2018)(Citation: Sophos SamSam Apr 2018)(Citation: Symantec SamSam Oct 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1027.016 Junk Code Insertion\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0691": "# S0691 Neoichor\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0691\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0691\n\n[Neoichor](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0691) is C2 malware used by [Ke3chang](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0004) since at least 2019; similar malware families used by the group include Leeson and Numbldea.(Citation: Microsoft NICKEL December 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1016.001 Internet Connection Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1614.001 System Language Discovery\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1559.001 Component Object Model\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1070 Indicator Removal\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0575": "# S0575 Conti\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0575\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0575\n\n[Conti](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0575) is a Ransomware-as-a-Service (RaaS) that was first observed in December 2019. [Conti](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0575) has been deployed via [TrickBot](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0266) and used against major corporations and government agencies, particularly those in North America. As with other ransomware families, actors using [Conti](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0575) steal sensitive files and information from compromised networks, and threaten to publish this data unless the ransom is paid.(Citation: Cybereason Conti Jan 2021)(Citation: CarbonBlack Conti July 2020)(Citation: Cybleinc Conti January 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1021.002 SMB/Windows Admin Shares\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1055.001 Dynamic-link Library Injection\n- T1080 Taint Shared Content\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1106 Native API\n", "mitre-attack-software-s9030": "# S9030 SameCoin\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S9030\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9030\n\n[SameCoin](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9030) is a multi-platform wiper with Windows and Android versions that has been used by [WIRTE](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0090) to target entities in the Middle East including in Israel.(Citation: Check Point Wirte NOV 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1534 Internal Spearphishing\n- T1485 Data Destruction\n- T1491.001 Internal Defacement\n- T1614 System Location Discovery\n- T1570 Lateral Tool Transfer\n- T1679 Selective Exclusion\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1130": "# S1130 Raspberry Robin\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1130\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1130\n\n[Raspberry Robin](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1130) is initial access malware first identified in September 2021, and active through early 2024. The malware is notable for spreading via infected USB devices containing a malicious LNK object that, on execution, retrieves remote hosted payloads for installation. [Raspberry Robin](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1130) has been widely used against various industries and geographies, and as a precursor to information stealer, ransomware, and other payloads such as [SocGholish](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1124), [Cobalt Strike](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0154), [IcedID](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0483), and [Bumblebee](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1039).(Citation: TrendMicro RaspberryRobin 2022)(Citation: RedCanary RaspberryRobin 2022)(Citation: HP RaspberryRobin 2024) The DLL componenet in the [Raspberry Robin](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1130) infection chain is also referred to as \"Roshtyak.\"(Citation: Avast RaspberryRobin 2022) The name \"Raspberry Robin\" is used to refer to both the malware as well as the threat actor associated with its use, although the Raspberry Robin operators are also tracked as <code>Storm-0856</code> by some vendors.(Citation: Microsoft RaspberryRobin 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1102 Web Service\n- T1480 Execution Guardrails\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1559 Inter-Process Communication\n- T1571 Non-Standard Port\n- T1497 Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1218.010 Regsvr32\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1574 Hijack Execution Flow\n- T1036.008 Masquerade File Type\n- T1091 Replication Through Removable Media\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1055.012 Process Hollowing\n- T1583.001 Domains\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1559.001 Component Object Model\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1218.008 Odbcconf\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1548 Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism\n- T1204 User Execution\n- T1622 Debugger Evasion\n- T1583.008 Malvertising\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1070.009 Clear Persistence\n- T1071 Application Layer Protocol\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1218.007 Msiexec\n- T1059 Command and Scripting Interpreter\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1122": "# S1122 Mispadu\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1122\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1122\n\n[Mispadu](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1122) is a banking trojan written in Delphi that was first observed in 2019 and uses a Malware-as-a-Service (MaaS) business model.(Citation: ESET Security Mispadu Facebook Ads 2019)(Citation: SCILabs Malteiro 2021) This malware is operated, managed, and sold by the [Malteiro](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1026) cybercriminal group.(Citation: SCILabs Malteiro 2021) [Mispadu](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1122) has mainly been used to target victims in Brazil and Mexico, and has also had confirmed operations throughout Latin America and Europe.(Citation: SCILabs Malteiro 2021)(Citation: SCILabs URSA/Mispadu Evolution 2023)(Citation: Segurança Informática URSA Sophisticated Loader 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1218.007 Msiexec\n- T1614.001 System Language Discovery\n- T1176.001 Browser Extensions\n- T1217 Browser Information Discovery\n- T1566.002 Spearphishing Link\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1056.002 GUI Input Capture\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1115 Clipboard Data\n- T1555 Credentials from Password Stores\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0166": "# S0166 RemoteCMD\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0166\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0166\n\n[RemoteCMD](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0166) is a custom tool used by [APT3](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0022) to execute commands on a remote system similar to SysInternal's PSEXEC functionality. (Citation: Symantec Buckeye)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1191": "# S1191 Megazord\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1191\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1191\n\n[Megazord](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1191) is a Rust-based variant of [Akira](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1129) ransomware that has been in use since at least August 2023 to target Windows environments. [Megazord](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1191) has been attributed to the [Akira](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1024) group based on overlapping infrastructure though is possibly not exclusive to the group.(Citation: CISA Akira Ransomware APR 2024)(Citation: Cisco Akira Ransomware OCT 2024)(Citation: Palo Alto Howling Scorpius DEC 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1654 Log Enumeration\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0659": "# S0659 Diavol\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0659\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0659\n\n[Diavol](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0659) is a ransomware variant first observed in June 2021 that is capable of prioritizing file types to encrypt based on a pre-configured list of extensions defined by the attacker.  The [Diavol](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0659) Ransomware-as-a Service (RaaS) program is managed by [Wizard Spider](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0102) and it has been observed being deployed by [Bazar](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0534).(Citation: Fortinet Diavol July 2021)(Citation: FBI Flash Diavol January 2022)(Citation: DFIR Diavol Ransomware December 2021)(Citation: Microsoft Ransomware as a Service)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1485 Data Destruction\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1021.002 SMB/Windows Admin Shares\n- T1491.001 Internal Defacement\n- T1027.003 Steganography\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1219": "# S1219 REPTILE\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1219\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1219\n\n[REPTILE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1219) is an open-source Linux rootkit with multiple components that provides backdoor access and functionality.(Citation: Google Cloud Mandiant UNC3886 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1543.004 Launch Daemon\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1205.001 Port Knocking\n- T1546.017 Udev Rules\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1205 Traffic Signaling\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1014 Rootkit\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1547.006 Kernel Modules and Extensions\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0565": "# S0565 Raindrop\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0565\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0565\n\n[Raindrop](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0565) is a loader used by [APT29](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0016) that was discovered on some victim machines during investigations related to the [SolarWinds Compromise](https://attack.mitre.org/campaigns/C0024). It was discovered in January 2021 and was likely used since at least May 2020.(Citation: Symantec RAINDROP January 2021)(Citation: Microsoft Deep Dive Solorigate January 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1497.003 Time Based Checks\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1036 Masquerading\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1027.003 Steganography\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0600": "# S0600 Doki\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0600\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0600\n\n[Doki](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0600) is a backdoor that uses a unique Dogecoin-based Domain Generation Algorithm and was first observed in July 2020. [Doki](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0600) was used in conjunction with the [ngrok](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0508) Mining Botnet in a campaign that targeted Docker servers in cloud platforms. (Citation: Intezer Doki July 20)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1568.002 Domain Generation Algorithms\n- T1611 Escape to Host\n- T1020 Automated Exfiltration\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1610 Deploy Container\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1102 Web Service\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1133 External Remote Services\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0146": "# S0146 TEXTMATE\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0146\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0146\n\n[TEXTMATE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0146) is a second-stage PowerShell backdoor that is memory-resident. It was observed being used along with [POWERSOURCE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0145) in February 2017. (Citation: FireEye FIN7 March 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0623": "# S0623 Siloscape\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0623\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0623\n\n[Siloscape](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0623) is malware that targets Kubernetes clusters through Windows containers. [Siloscape](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0623) was first observed in March 2021.(Citation: Unit 42 Siloscape Jun 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1190 Exploit Public-Facing Application\n- T1090.003 Multi-hop Proxy\n- T1611 Escape to Host\n- T1071 Application Layer Protocol\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1068 Exploitation for Privilege Escalation\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1609 Container Administration Command\n- T1069 Permission Groups Discovery\n- T1134.001 Token Impersonation/Theft\n- T1106 Native API\n", "mitre-attack-software-s9037": "# S9037 RustyWater\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S9037\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9037\n\n[RustyWater](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9037) is a Rust-based implant used by [MuddyWater](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0069). Historically, [MuddyWater](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0069) has used PowerShell-based tools and [RustyWater](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9037) reflects a shift in tooling, demonstrating better techniques for defense evasion and reverse engineering.(Citation: CloudSEK_RustyWater_Jan2026)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1684.001 Impersonation\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1055.002 Portable Ex