scovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0608": "# S0608 Conficker\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0608\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0608\n\n[Conficker](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0608) is a computer worm first detected in October 2008 that targeted Microsoft Windows using the MS08-067 Windows vulnerability to spread.(Citation: SANS Conficker) In 2016, a variant of [Conficker](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0608) made its way on computers and removable disk drives belonging to a nuclear power plant.(Citation: Conficker Nuclear Power Plant)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1021.002 SMB/Windows Admin Shares\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1568.002 Domain Generation Algorithms\n- T1210 Exploitation of Remote Services\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1046 Network Service Discovery\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1091 Replication Through Removable Media\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1124": "# S1124 SocGholish\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1124\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1124\n\n[SocGholish](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1124) is a JavaScript-based loader malware that has been used since at least 2017. It has been observed in use against multiple sectors globally for initial access, primarily through drive-by-downloads masquerading as software updates. SocGholish is operated by [Mustard Tempest](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1020) and its access has been sold to groups including [Indrik Spider](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0119) for downloading secondary RAT and ransomware payloads.(Citation: SentinelOne SocGholish Infrastructure November 2022)(Citation: SocGholish-update)(Citation: Red Canary SocGholish March 2024)(Citation: Secureworks Gold Prelude Profile)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1614 System Location Discovery\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1482 Domain Trust Discovery\n- T1059.007 JavaScript\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1566.002 Spearphishing Link\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1189 Drive-by Compromise\n- T1204.001 Malicious Link\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1048.003 Exfiltration Over Unencrypted Non-C2 Protocol\n- T1102 Web Service\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1027.015 Compression\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0696": "# S0696 Flagpro\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0696\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0696\n\n[Flagpro](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0696) is a Windows-based, first-stage downloader that has been used by [BlackTech](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0098) since at least October 2020. It has primarily been used against defense, media, and communications companies in Japan.(Citation: NTT Security Flagpro new December 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1010 Application Window Discovery\n- T1029 Scheduled Transfer\n- T1614.001 System Language Discovery\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1036 Masquerading\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1069.001 Local Groups\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n- T1070 Indicator Removal\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0087": "# S0087 Hi-Zor\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0087\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0087\n\n[Hi-Zor](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0087) is a remote access tool (RAT) that has characteristics similar to [Sakula](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0074). It was used in a campaign named INOCNATION. (Citation: Fidelis Hi-Zor)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1218.010 Regsvr32\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0646": "# S0646 SpicyOmelette\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0646\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0646\n\n[SpicyOmelette](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0646) is a JavaScript based remote access tool that has been used by [Cobalt Group](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0080) since at least 2018.(Citation: Secureworks GOLD KINGSWOOD September 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1566.002 Spearphishing Link\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1059.007 JavaScript\n- T1204.001 Malicious Link\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0161": "# S0161 XAgentOSX\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0161\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0161\n\n[XAgentOSX](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0161) is a trojan that has been used by [APT28](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0007)  on OS X and appears to be a port of their standard [CHOPSTICK](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0023) or XAgent trojan. (Citation: XAgentOSX 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1071.002 File Transfer Protocols\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1106 Native API\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0690": "# S0690 Green Lambert\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0690\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0690\n\n[Green Lambert](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0690) is a modular backdoor that security researchers assess has been used by an advanced threat group referred to as Longhorn and The Lamberts. First reported in 2017, the Windows variant of [Green Lambert](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0690) may have been used as early as 2008; a macOS version was uploaded to a multiscanner service in September 2014.(Citation: Kaspersky Lamberts Toolkit April 2017)(Citation: Objective See Green Lambert for OSX Oct 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1543.004 Launch Daemon\n- T1555.001 Keychain\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1090 Proxy\n- T1547.015 Login Items\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1546.004 Unix Shell Configuration Modification\n- T1543.001 Launch Agent\n- T1037.004 RC Scripts\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0020": "# S0020 China Chopper\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0020\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0020\n\n[China Chopper](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0020) is a [Web Shell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1505/003) hosted on Web servers to provide access back into an enterprise network that does not rely on an infected system calling back to a remote command and control server.(Citation: Lee 2013) It has been used by several threat groups.(Citation: Dell TG-3390)(Citation: FireEye Periscope March 2018)(Citation: CISA AA21-200A APT40 July 2021)(Citation: Rapid7 HAFNIUM Mar 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1110.001 Password Guessing\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1046 Network Service Discovery\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n- T1505.003 Web Shell\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1163": "# S1163 SnappyTCP\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1163\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1163\n\n[SnappyTCP](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1163) is a web shell used by [Sea Turtle](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1041) between 2021 and 2023 against multiple victims. [SnappyTCP](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1163) appears to be based on a public GitHub project that has since been removed from the code-sharing site. [SnappyTCP](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1163) includes a simple reverse TCP shell for Linux and Unix environments with basic command and control capabilities.(Citation: PWC Sea Turtle 2023)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1505.003 Web Shell\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0025": "# S0025 CALENDAR\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0025\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0025\n\n[CALENDAR](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0025) is malware used by [APT1](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0006) that mimics legitimate Gmail Calendar traffic. (Citation: Mandiant APT1)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1102.002 Bidirectional Communication\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0372": "# S0372 LockerGoga\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0372\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0372\n\n[LockerGoga](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0372) is ransomware that was first reported in January 2019, and has been tied to various attacks on European companies, including industrial and manufacturing firms.(Citation: Unit42 LockerGoga 2019)(Citation: CarbonBlack LockerGoga 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1529 System Shutdown/Reboot\n- T1531 Account Access Removal\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n- T1570 Lateral Tool Transfer\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1185": "# S1185 LightSpy\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1185\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1185\n\nFirst observed in 2018, LightSpy is a modular malware family that initially targeted iOS devices in Southern Asia before expanding to Android and macOS platforms. It consists of a downloader, a main executable that manages network communications, and functionality-specific modules, typically implemented as `.dylib` files (iOS, macOS) or `.apk` files (Android). LightSpy can collect VoIP call recordings, SMS messages, and credential stores, which are then exfiltrated to a command and control (C2) server.(Citation: MelikovBlackBerry LightSpy 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1480 Execution Guardrails\n- T1555.001 Keychain\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1129 Shared Modules\n- T1123 Audio Capture\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1217 Browser Information Discovery\n- T1027.001 Binary Padding\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1046 Network Service Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0220": "# S0220 Chaos\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0220\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0220\n\n[Chaos](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0220) is Linux malware that compromises systems by brute force attacks against SSH services. Once installed, it provides a reverse shell to its controllers, triggered by unsolicited packets. (Citation: Chaos Stolen Backdoor)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1110 Brute Force\n- T1205 Traffic Signaling\n- T1104 Multi-Stage Channels\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0189": "# S0189 ISMInjector\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0189\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0189\n\n[ISMInjector](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0189) is a Trojan used to install another [OilRig](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0049) backdoor, ISMAgent. (Citation: OilRig New Delivery Oct 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1055.012 Process Hollowing\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0196": "# S0196 PUNCHBUGGY\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0196\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0196\n\n[PUNCHBUGGY](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0196) is a backdoor malware used by [FIN8](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0061) that has been observed targeting POS networks in the hospitality industry. (Citation: Morphisec ShellTea June 2019)(Citation: FireEye Fin8 May 2016) (Citation: FireEye Know Your Enemy FIN8 Aug 2016)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1059.006 Python\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1546.009 AppCert DLLs\n- T1129 Shared Modules\n- T1087.001 Local Account\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1560.001 Archive via Utility\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0588": "# S0588 GoldMax\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0588\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0588\n\n[GoldMax](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0588) is a second-stage C2 backdoor written in Go with Windows and Linux variants that are nearly identical in functionality. [GoldMax](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0588) was discovered in early 2021 during the investigation into the [SolarWinds Compromise](https://attack.mitre.org/campaigns/C0024), and has likely been used by [APT29](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0016) since at least mid-2019. [GoldMax](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0588) uses multiple defense evasion techniques, including avoiding virtualization execution and masking malicious traffic.(Citation: MSTIC NOBELIUM Mar 2021)(Citation: FireEye SUNSHUTTLE Mar 2021)(Citation: CrowdStrike StellarParticle January 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1564.011 Ignore Process Interrupts\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1053.003 Cron\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1001.001 Junk Data\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1497.003 Time Based Checks\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0617": "# S0617 HELLOKITTY\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0617\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0617\n\n[HELLOKITTY](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0617) is a ransomware written in C++  that shares similar code structure and functionality with [DEATHRANSOM](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0616) and [FIVEHANDS](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0618). [HELLOKITTY](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0617) has been used since at least 2020, targets have included a Polish video game developer and a Brazilian electric power company.(Citation: FireEye FiveHands April 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0614": "# S0614 CostaBricks\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0614\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0614\n\n[CostaBricks](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0614) is a loader that was used to deploy 32-bit backdoors in the [CostaRicto](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0132) campaign.(Citation: BlackBerry CostaRicto November 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1027.001 Binary Padding\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1096": "# S1096 Cheerscrypt\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1096\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1096\n\n[Cheerscrypt](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1096) is a ransomware that was developed by [Cinnamon Tempest](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1021) and has been used in attacks against ESXi and Windows environments since at least 2022. [Cheerscrypt](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1096) was derived from the leaked [Babuk](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0638) source code and has infrastructure overlaps with deployments of Night Sky ransomware, which was also derived from [Babuk](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0638).(Citation: Sygnia Emperor Dragonfly October 2022)(Citation: Trend Micro Cheerscrypt May 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1059.012 Hypervisor CLI\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1673 Virtual Machine Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1119": "# S1119 LIGHTWIRE\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1119\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1119\n\n[LIGHTWIRE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1119) is a web shell written in Perl that was used during [Cutting Edge](https://attack.mitre.org/campaigns/C0029) to maintain access and enable command execution by imbedding into the legitimate compcheckresult.cgi component of Ivanti Secure Connect VPNs.(Citation: Mandiant Cutting Edge Part 2 January 2024)(Citation: Mandiant Cutting Edge January 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1505.003 Web Shell\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1554 Compromise Host Software Binary\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0387": "# S0387 KeyBoy\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0387\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0387\n\n[KeyBoy](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0387) is malware that has been used in targeted campaigns against members of the Tibetan Parliament in 2016.(Citation: CitizenLab KeyBoy Nov 2016)(Citation: PWC KeyBoys Feb 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1059.006 Python\n- T1564.003 Hidden Window\n- T1001.003 Protocol or Service Impersonation\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1559.002 Dynamic Data Exchange\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1547.004 Winlogon Helper DLL\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0150": "# S0150 POSHSPY\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0150\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0150\n\n[POSHSPY](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0150) is a backdoor that has been used by [APT29](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0016) since at least 2015. It appears to be used as a secondary backdoor used if the actors lost access to their primary backdoors. (Citation: FireEye POSHSPY April 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1030 Data Transfer Size Limits\n- T1568.002 Domain Generation Algorithms\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n- T1546.003 Windows Management Instrumentation Event Subscription\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0051": "# S0051 MiniDuke\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0051\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0051\n\n[MiniDuke](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0051) is malware that was used by [APT29](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0016) from 2010 to 2015. The [MiniDuke](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0051) toolset consists of multiple downloader and backdoor components. The loader has been used with other [MiniDuke](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0051) components as well as in conjunction with [CosmicDuke](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0050) and [PinchDuke](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0048). (Citation: F-Secure The Dukes)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1102.001 Dead Drop Resolver\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1568.002 Domain Generation Algorithms\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1008 Fallback Channels\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1090.001 Internal Proxy\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0398": "# S0398 HyperBro\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0398\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0398\n\n[HyperBro](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0398) is a custom in-memory backdoor used by [Threat Group-3390](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0027).(Citation: Unit42 Emissary Panda May 2019)(Citation: Securelist LuckyMouse June 2018)(Citation: Hacker News LuckyMouse June 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0504": "# S0504 Anchor\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0504\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0504\n\n[Anchor](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0504) is one of a family of backdoor malware that has been used in conjunction with [TrickBot](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0266) on selected high profile targets since at least 2018.(Citation: Cyberreason Anchor December 2019)(Citation: Medium Anchor DNS July 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1480 Execution Guardrails\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1053.003 Cron\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1564.004 NTFS File Attributes\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1021.002 SMB/Windows Admin Shares\n- T1008 Fallback Channels\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1188": "# S1188 Line Runner\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1188\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1188\n\n[Line Runner](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1188) is a persistent backdoor and web shell allowing threat actors to upload and execute arbitrary Lua scripts. [Line Runner](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1188) is associated with the [ArcaneDoor](https://attack.mitre.org/campaigns/C0046) campaign.(Citation: CCCS ArcaneDoor 2024)(Citation: Cisco ArcaneDoor 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1027.015 Compression\n- T1653 Power Settings\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1059.011 Lua\n- T1505.003 Web Shell\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1557 Adversary-in-the-Middle\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0147": "# S0147 Pteranodon\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0147\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0147\n\n[Pteranodon](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0147) is a custom backdoor used by [Gamaredon Group](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0047). (Citation: Palo Alto Gamaredon Feb 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1027.007 Dynamic API Resolution\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1497 Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion\n- T1218.005 Mshta\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1066": "# S1066 DarkTortilla\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1066\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1066\n\n[DarkTortilla](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1066) is a highly configurable .NET-based crypter that has been possibly active since at least August 2015. [DarkTortilla](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1066) has been used to deliver popular information stealers, RATs, and payloads such as [Agent Tesla](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0331), AsyncRat, [NanoCore](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0336), RedLine, [Cobalt Strike](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0154), and Metasploit.(Citation: Secureworks DarkTortilla Aug 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1055.001 Dynamic-link Library Injection\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1547.004 Winlogon Helper DLL\n- T1497.003 Time Based Checks\n- T1574.012 COR_PROFILER\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1036 Masquerading\n- T1564 Hide Artifacts\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1115 Clipboard Data\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1016.001 Internet Connection Discovery\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1102 Web Service\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1559.001 Component Object Model\n- T1622 Debugger Evasion\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1246": "# S1246 BeaverTail\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1246\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1246\n\n[BeaverTail](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1246) is a malware that has both a JavaScript and C++ variant.  Active since 2022, [BeaverTail](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1246) is capable of stealing logins from browsers and serves as a downloader for second stage payloads. [BeaverTail](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1246) has previously been leveraged by North Korea-affiliated actors identified as DeceptiveDevelopment or [Contagious Interview](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1052). [BeaverTail](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1246) has been delivered to victims through code repository sites and has been embedded within malicious attachments.(Citation: PaloAlto ContagiousInterview BeaverTail InvisibleFerret November 2023)(Citation: Esentire ContagiousInterview BeaverTail InvisibleFerret November 2024)(Citation: ESET Contagious Interview BeaverTail InvisibleFerret February 2025)(Citation: Zscaler ContagiousInterview BeaverTail InvisibleFerret November 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1059.007 JavaScript\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1654 Log Enumeration\n- T1195.001 Compromise Software Dependencies and Development Tools\n- T1217 Browser Information Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1560.001 Archive via Utility\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1571 Non-Standard Port\n- T1555 Credentials from Password Stores\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1001.001 Junk Data\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1036 Masquerading\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1657 Financial Theft\n- T1555.001 Keychain\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0240": "# S0240 ROKRAT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0240\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0240\n\n[ROKRAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0240) is a cloud-based remote access tool (RAT) used by [APT37](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0067) to target victims in South Korea. [APT37](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0067) has used ROKRAT during several campaigns from 2016 through 2021.(Citation: Talos ROKRAT)(Citation: Talos Group123)(Citation: Volexity InkySquid RokRAT August 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1123 Audio Capture\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1622 Debugger Evasion\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1555.004 Windows Credential Manager\n- T1480.001 Environmental Keying\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n- T1115 Clipboard Data\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1102.002 Bidirectional Communication\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1010 Application Window Discovery\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1567.002 Exfiltration to Cloud Storage\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0137": "# S0137 CORESHELL\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0137\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0137\n\n[CORESHELL](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0137) is a downloader used by [APT28](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0007). The older versions of this malware are known as SOURFACE and newer versions as CORESHELL.(Citation: FireEye APT28) (Citation: FireEye APT28 January 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1071.003 Mail Protocols\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1027.016 Junk Code Insertion\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0253": "# S0253 RunningRAT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0253\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0253\n\n[RunningRAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0253) is a remote access tool that appeared in operations surrounding the 2018 Pyeongchang Winter Olympics along with [Gold Dragon](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0249) and [Brave Prince](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0252). (Citation: McAfee Gold Dragon)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1560 Archive Collected Data\n- T1685.005 Clear Windows Event Logs\n- T1115 Clipboard Data\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1010": "# S1010 VPNFilter\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1010\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1010\n\n[VPNFilter](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1010) is a multi-stage, modular platform with versatile capabilities to support both intelligence-collection and destructive cyber attack operations. [VPNFilter](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1010) modules such as its packet sniffer ('ps') can collect traffic that passes through an infected device, allowing the theft of website credentials and monitoring of Modbus SCADA protocols. (Citation: William Largent June 2018) (Citation: Carl Hurd March 2019) [VPNFilter](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1010) was assessed to be replaced by [Sandworm Team](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0034) with [Cyclops Blink](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0687) starting in 2019.(Citation: NCSC CISA Cyclops Blink Advisory February 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1561.001 Disk Content Wipe\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1232": "# S1232 SplatDropper\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1232\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1232\n\n[SplatDropper](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1232) is a loader that utilizes native windows API to deliver its payload to the victim environment.  [SplatDropper](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1232) has been delivered through RAR archives and used legitimate executable for DLL side-loading.  [SplatDropper](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1232) is known to be leveraged by [Mustang Panda](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0129) and was first observed utilized in 2025.\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1027.007 Dynamic API Resolution\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1070.009 Clear Persistence\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0638": "# S0638 Babuk\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0638\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0638\n\n[Babuk](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0638) is a Ransomware-as-a-service (RaaS) malware that has been used since at least 2021. The operators of [Babuk](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0638) employ a \"Big Game Hunting\" approach to targeting major enterprises and operate a leak site to post stolen data as part of their extortion scheme.(Citation: Sogeti CERT ESEC Babuk March 2021)(Citation: McAfee Babuk February 2021)(Citation: CyberScoop Babuk February 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1179": "# S1179 Exbyte\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1179\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1179\n\n[Exbyte](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1179) is an exfiltration tool written in Go that is uniquely associated with [BlackByte](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1043) operations. Observed since 2022, [Exbyte](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1179) transfers collected files to online file sharing and hosting services.(Citation: Symantec BlackByte 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1480 Execution Guardrails\n- T1069.001 Local Groups\n- T1140 Deobfuscat