oftware/S1247) is a ransomware variant written in Rust that has been active since at least May 2024.(Citation: Cyble Embargo Ransomware May 2024)(Citation: ESET Embargo Ransomware October 2024)  [Embargo](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1247) ransomware operations are associated with “double extortion” ransomware activity, where data is exfiltrated from victim environments prior to encryption, with threats to publish files if a ransom is not paid.(Citation: Cyble Embargo Ransomware May 2024)(Citation: ESET Embargo Ransomware October 2024)  [Embargo](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1247) ransomware has been known to be delivered through a loader known as MDeployer which also leverages a malware component known as MS4Killer that facilitates termination of processes operating on the victim hosts.(Citation: ESET Embargo Ransomware October 2024) [Embargo](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1247) is also reportedly a Ransomware as a Service (RaaS).(Citation: ESET Embargo Ransomware October 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1480.002 Mutual Exclusion\n- T1068 Exploitation for Privilege Escalation\n- T1657 Financial Theft\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1688 Safe Mode Boot\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1679 Selective Exclusion\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0094": "# S0094 Trojan.Karagany\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0094\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0094\n\n[Trojan.Karagany](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0094) is a modular remote access tool used for recon and linked to [Dragonfly](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0035). The source code for [Trojan.Karagany](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0094) originated from Dream Loader malware which was leaked in 2010 and sold on underground forums. (Citation: Symantec Dragonfly)(Citation: Secureworks Karagany July 2019)(Citation: Dragos DYMALLOY )\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1010 Application Window Discovery\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1055.003 Thread Execution Hijacking\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1003 OS Credential Dumping\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0234": "# S0234 Bandook\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0234\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0234\n\n[Bandook](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0234) is a commercially available RAT, written in Delphi and C++, that has been available since at least 2007. It has been used against government, financial, energy, healthcare, education, IT, and legal organizations in the US, South America, Europe, and Southeast Asia. [Bandook](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0234) has been used by [Dark Caracal](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0070), as well as in a separate campaign referred to as \"Operation Manul\".(Citation: EFF Manul Aug 2016)(Citation: Lookout Dark Caracal Jan 2018)(Citation: CheckPoint Bandook Nov 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1123 Audio Capture\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1055.012 Process Hollowing\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1120 Peripheral Device Discovery\n- T1027.003 Steganography\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n- T1059 Command and Scripting Interpreter\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1125 Video Capture\n- T1059.006 Python\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0501": "# S0501 PipeMon\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0501\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0501\n\n[PipeMon](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0501) is a multi-stage modular backdoor used by [Winnti Group](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0044).(Citation: ESET PipeMon May 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1134.002 Create Process with Token\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1055.001 Dynamic-link Library Injection\n- T1547.012 Print Processors\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1134.004 Parent PID Spoofing\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1027.011 Fileless Storage\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n- T1008 Fallback Channels\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1129 Shared Modules\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0519": "# S0519 SYNful Knock\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0519\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0519\n\n[SYNful Knock](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0519) is a stealthy modification of the operating system of network devices that can be used to maintain persistence within a victim's network and provide new capabilities to the adversary.(Citation: Mandiant - Synful Knock)(Citation: Cisco Synful Knock Evolution)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1601.001 Patch System Image\n- T1205 Traffic Signaling\n- T1556.004 Network Device Authentication\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1182": "# S1182 MagicRAT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1182\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1182\n\n[MagicRAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1182) is a remote access tool developed in C++ and exclusively used by the [Lazarus Group](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0032) threat actor in operations. [MagicRAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1182) allows for arbitrary command execution on victim machines and provides basic remote access functionality.(Citation: Cisco MagicRAT 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1036.008 Masquerade File Type\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0131": "# S0131 TINYTYPHON\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0131\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0131\n\n[TINYTYPHON](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0131) is a backdoor  that has been used by the actors responsible for the MONSOON campaign. The majority of its code was reportedly taken from the MyDoom worm. (Citation: Forcepoint Monsoon)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1020 Automated Exfiltration\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0356": "# S0356 KONNI\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0356\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0356\n\n[KONNI](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0356) is a remote access tool that security researchers assess has been used by North Korean cyber actors since at least 2014. [KONNI](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0356) has significant code overlap with the [NOKKI](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0353) malware family, and has been linked to several suspected North Korean campaigns targeting political organizations in Russia, East Asia, Europe and the Middle East; there is some evidence potentially linking [KONNI](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0356) to [APT37](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0067).(Citation: Talos Konni May 2017)(Citation: Unit 42 NOKKI Sept 2018)(Citation: Unit 42 Nokki Oct 2018)(Citation: Medium KONNI Jan 2020)(Citation: Malwarebytes Konni Aug 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1546.015 Component Object Model Hijacking\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1048.003 Exfiltration Over Unencrypted Non-C2 Protocol\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1134.004 Parent PID Spoofing\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1115 Clipboard Data\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1560 Archive Collected Data\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1134.002 Create Process with Token\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1547.009 Shortcut Modification\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1059.007 JavaScript\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0098": "# S0098 T9000\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0098\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0098\n\n[T9000](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0098) is a backdoor that is a newer variant of the T5000 malware family, also known as Plat1. Its primary function is to gather information about the victim. It has been used in multiple targeted attacks against U.S.-based organizations. (Citation: FireEye admin@338 March 2014) (Citation: Palo Alto T9000 Feb 2016)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1119 Automated Collection\n- T1546.010 AppInit DLLs\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1123 Audio Capture\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1560.003 Archive via Custom Method\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1125 Video Capture\n- T1120 Peripheral Device Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0430": "# S0430 Winnti for Linux\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0430\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0430\n\n[Winnti for Linux](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0430) is a trojan, seen since at least 2015, designed specifically for targeting Linux systems. Reporting indicates the winnti malware family is shared across a number of actors including [Winnti Group](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0044). The Windows variant is tracked separately under [Winnti for Windows](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0141).(Citation: Chronicle Winnti for Linux May 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1205 Traffic Signaling\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1014 Rootkit\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1113": "# S1113 RAPIDPULSE\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1113\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1113\n\n[RAPIDPULSE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1113) is a web shell that exists as a modification to a legitimate Pulse Secure file that has been used by [APT5](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1023) since at least 2021.(Citation: Mandiant Pulse Secure Update May 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1505.003 Web Shell\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0032": "# S0032 gh0st RAT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0032\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0032\n\n[gh0st RAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0032) is a remote access tool (RAT). The source code is public and it has been used by multiple groups.(Citation: FireEye Hacking Team)(Citation: Arbor Musical Chairs Feb 2018)(Citation: Nccgroup Gh0st April 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1129 Shared Modules\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1685.005 Clear Windows Event Logs\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1059 Command and Scripting Interpreter\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1568.001 Fast Flux DNS\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1573 Encrypted Channel\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0140": "# S0140 Shamoon\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0140\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0140\n\n[Shamoon](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0140) is wiper malware that was first used by an Iranian group known as the \"Cutting Sword of Justice\" in 2012. Other versions known as Shamoon 2 and Shamoon 3 were observed in 2016 and 2018. [Shamoon](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0140) has also been seen leveraging [RawDisk](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0364) and Filerase to carry out data wiping tasks. Analysis has linked [Shamoon](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0140) with [Kwampirs](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0236) based on multiple shared artifacts and coding patterns.(Citation: Cylera Kwampirs 2022) The term Shamoon is sometimes used to refer to the group using the malware as well as the malware itself.(Citation: Palo Alto Shamoon Nov 2016)(Citation: Unit 42 Shamoon3 2018)(Citation: Symantec Shamoon 2012)(Citation: FireEye Shamoon Nov 2016)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1561.002 Disk Structure Wipe\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1021.002 SMB/Windows Admin Shares\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1485 Data Destruction\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1078.002 Domain Accounts\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1134.001 Token Impersonation/Theft\n- T1570 Lateral Tool Transfer\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1529 System Shutdown/Reboot\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0007": "# S0007 Skeleton Key\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0007\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0007\n\n[Skeleton Key](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0007) is malware used to inject false credentials into domain controllers with the intent of creating a backdoor password. (Citation: Dell Skeleton) Functionality similar to [Skeleton Key](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0007) is included as a module in [Mimikatz](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0002).\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1556.001 Domain Controller Authentication\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1021": "# S1021 DnsSystem\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1021\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1021\n\n[DnsSystem](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1021) is a .NET based DNS backdoor, which is a customized version of the open source tool DIG.net, that has been used by [HEXANE](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1001) since at least June 2022.(Citation: Zscaler Lyceum DnsSystem June 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0553": "# S0553 MoleNet\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0553\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0553\n\n[MoleNet](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0553) is a downloader tool with backdoor capabilities that has been observed in use since at least 2019.(Citation: Cybereason Molerats Dec 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0212": "# S0212 CORALDECK\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0212\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0212\n\n[CORALDECK](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0212) is an exfiltration tool used by [APT37](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0067). (Citation: FireEye APT37 Feb 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1048.003 Exfiltration Over Unencrypted Non-C2 Protocol\n- T1560.001 Archive via Utility\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0044": "# S0044 JHUHUGIT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0044\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0044\n\n[JHUHUGIT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0044) is malware used by [APT28](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0007). It is based on Carberp source code and serves as reconnaissance malware. (Citation: Kaspersky Sofacy) (Citation: F-Secure Sofacy 2015) (Citation: ESET Sednit Part 1) (Citation: FireEye APT28 January 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1008 Fallback Channels\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1037.001 Logon Script (Windows)\n- T1115 Clipboard Data\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1068 Exploitation for Privilege Escalation\n- T1546.015 Component Object Model Hijacking\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0035": "# S0035 SPACESHIP\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0035\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0035\n\n[SPACESHIP](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0035) is malware developed by [APT30](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0013) that allows propagation and exfiltration of data over removable devices. [APT30](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0013) may use this capability to exfiltrate data across air-gaps. (Citation: FireEye APT30)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1052.001 Exfiltration over USB\n- T1560.003 Archive via Custom Method\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1547.009 Shortcut Modification\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0657": "# S0657 BLUELIGHT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0657\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0657\n\n[BLUELIGHT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0657) is a remote access Trojan used by [APT37](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0067) that was first observed in early 2021.(Citation: Volexity InkySquid BLUELIGHT August 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1560.003 Archive via Custom Method\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1102.002 Bidirectional Communication\n- T1539 Steal Web Session Cookie\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1560 Archive Collected Data\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0526": "# S0526 KGH_SPY\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0526\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0526\n\n[KGH_SPY](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0526) is a modular suite of tools used by [Kimsuky](h