d features a module design with multiple available plugins that have been under active development through 2024.(Citation: Szappanos MgBot 2014)(Citation: ESET EvasivePanda 2023)(Citation: Symantec Daggerfly 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1087.002 Domain Account\n- T1003 OS Credential Dumping\n- T1555 Credentials from Password Stores\n- T1087.001 Local Account\n- T1213.006 Databases\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1025 Data from Removable Media\n- T1482 Domain Trust Discovery\n- T1115 Clipboard Data\n- T1539 Steal Web Session Cookie\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1046 Network Service Discovery\n- T1123 Audio Capture\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1043": "# S1043 ccf32\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1043\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1043\n\n[ccf32](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1043) is data collection malware that has been used since at least February 2019, most notably during the [FunnyDream](https://attack.mitre.org/campaigns/C0007) campaign; there is also a similar x64 version.(Citation: Bitdefender FunnyDream Campaign November 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1119 Automated Collection\n- T1048.003 Exfiltration Over Unencrypted Non-C2 Protocol\n- T1074.002 Remote Data Staging\n- T1560.001 Archive via Utility\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1190": "# S1190 Kapeka\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1190\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1190\n\nKapeka is a backdoor written in C++ used against victims in Eastern Europe since at least mid-2022. Kapeka has technical overlaps with [Exaramel for Windows](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0343) and [Prestige](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1058) malware variants, both of which are linked to [Sandworm Team](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0034). Kapeka may have been used in advance of [Prestige](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1058) deployment in late 2022.(Citation: WithSecure Kapeka 2024)(Citation: Microsoft KnuckleTouch 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1036.008 Masquerade File Type\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1070.009 Clear Persistence\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1090 Proxy\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1199": "# S1199 LockBit 2.0\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1199\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1199\n\n[LockBit 2.0](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1199) is an affiliate-based Ransomware-as-a-Service (RaaS) that has been in use since at least June 2021 as the successor to LockBit Ransomware. [LockBit 2.0](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1199) has versions capable of infecting Windows and VMware ESXi virtual machines, and has been observed targeting multiple industry verticals globally.(Citation: FBI Lockbit 2.0 FEB 2022)(Citation: Palo Alto Lockbit 2.0 JUN 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1564.003 Hidden Window\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1136 Create Account\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1484.001 Group Policy Modification\n- T1120 Peripheral Device Discovery\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1685.005 Clear Windows Event Logs\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1480 Execution Guardrails\n- T1614.001 System Language Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1021.002 SMB/Windows Admin Shares\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1171": "# S1171 OilCheck\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1171\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1171\n\n[OilCheck](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1171) is a C#/.NET downloader that has been used by [OilRig](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0049) since at least 2022 including against targets in Israel. [OilCheck](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1171) uses draft messages created in a shared email account for C2 communication.(Citation: ESET OilRig Downloaders DEC 2023)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1567 Exfiltration Over Web Service\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1102.002 Bidirectional Communication\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0251": "# S0251 Zebrocy\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0251\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0251\n\n[Zebrocy](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0251) is a Trojan that has been used by [APT28](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0007) since at least November 2015. The malware comes in several programming language variants, including C++, Delphi, AutoIt, C#, VB.NET, and Golang. (Citation: Palo Alto Sofacy 06-2018)(Citation: Unit42 Cannon Nov 2018)(Citation: Unit42 Sofacy Dec 2018)(Citation: CISA Zebrocy Oct 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1120 Peripheral Device Discovery\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1056.004 Credential API Hoo