 ThreatFabric_Crocodilus_March2025)(Citation: ThreatFabric_Crocodilus_June2025)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1657 Financial Theft\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0492": "# S0492 CookieMiner\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0492\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0492\n\n[CookieMiner](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0492) is mac-based malware that targets information associated with cryptocurrency exchanges as well as enabling cryptocurrency mining on the victim system itself. It was first discovered in the wild in 2019.(Citation: Unit42 CookieMiner Jan 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1048.003 Exfiltration Over Unencrypted Non-C2 Protocol\n- T1496.001 Compute Hijacking\n- T1539 Steal Web Session Cookie\n- T1686 Disable or Modify System Firewall\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1027.010 Command Obfuscation\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1543.001 Launch Agent\n- T1059.006 Python\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0499": "# S0499 Hancitor\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0499\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0499\n\n[Hancitor](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0499) is a downloader that has been used by [Pony](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0453) and other information stealing malware.(Citation: Threatpost Hancitor)(Citation: FireEye Hancitor)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1218.012 Verclsid\n- T1027.015 Compression\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1204.001 Malicious Link\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1566.002 Spearphishing Link\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1497 Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0666": "# S0666 Gelsemium\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0666\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0666\n\n[Gelsemium](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0666) is a modular malware comprised of a dropper (Gelsemine), a loader (Gelsenicine), and main (Gelsevirine) plug-ins written using the Microsoft Foundation Class (MFC) framework. [Gelsemium](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0666) has been used by the Gelsemium group since at least 2014.(Citation: ESET Gelsemium June 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1568 Dynamic Resolution\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1620 Reflective Code Loading\n- T1547.012 Print Processors\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1497 Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion\n- T1027.011 Fileless Storage\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1008 Fallback Channels\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1055.001 Dynamic-link Library Injection\n- T1134 Access Token Manipulation\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1036.001 Invalid Code Signature\n- T1027.016 Junk Code Insertion\n- T1027.015 Compression\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1559.001 Component Object Model\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0283": "# S0283 jRAT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0283\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0283\n\n[jRAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0283) is a cross-platform, Java-based backdoor originally available for purchase in 2012. Variants of [jRAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0283) have been distributed via a software-as-a-service platform, similar to an online subscription model.(Citation: Kaspersky Adwind Feb 2016) (Citation: jRAT Symantec Aug 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1120 Peripheral Device Discovery\n- T1029 Scheduled Transfer\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1125 Video Capture\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1037.005 Startup Items\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1021.001 Remote Desktop Protocol\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1090 Proxy\n- T1059.007 JavaScript\n- T1115 Clipboard Data\n- T1552.001 Credentials In Files\n- T1123 Audio Capture\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1552.004 Private Keys\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0170": "# S0170 Helminth\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0170\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0170\n\n[Helminth](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0170) is a backdoor that has at least two variants - one written in VBScript and PowerShell that is delivered via a macros in Excel spreadsheets, and one that is a standalone Windows executable. (Citation: Palo Alto OilRig May 2016)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1547.009 Shortcut Modification\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1115 Clipboard Data\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1069.001 Local Groups\n- T1069.002 Domain Groups\n- T1119 Automated Collection\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1030 Data Transfer Size Limits\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0384": "# S0384 Dridex\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0384\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0384\n\n[Dridex](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0384) is a prolific banking Trojan that first appeared in 2014. By December 2019, the US Treasury estimated [Dridex](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0384) had infected computers in hundreds of banks and financial institutions in over 40 countries, leading to more than $100 million in theft. [Dridex](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0384) was created from the source code of the Bugat banking Trojan (also known as Cridex).(Citation: Dell Dridex Oct 2015)(Citation: Kaspersky Dridex May 2017)(Citation: Treasury EvilCorp Dec 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1219 Remote Access Tools\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1185 Browser Session Hijacking\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1218.010 Regsvr32\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1090 Proxy\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1090.003 Multi-hop Proxy\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0470": "# S0470 BBK\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0470\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0470\n\n[BBK](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0470) is a downloader that has been used by [BRONZE BUTLER](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0060) since at least 2019.(Citation: Trend Micro Tick November 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1027.003 Steganography\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1106 Native API\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0162": "# S0162 Komplex\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0162\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0162\n\n[Komplex](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0162) is a backdoor that has been used by [APT28](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0007) on OS X and appears to be developed in a similar manner to [XAgentOSX](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0161) (Citation: XAgentOSX 2017) (Citation: Sofacy Komplex Trojan).\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1543.001 Launch Agent\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0402": "# S0402 OSX/Shlayer\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0402\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0402\n\n[OSX/Shlayer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0402) is a Trojan designed to install adware on macOS that was first discovered in 2018.(Citation: Carbon Black Shlayer Feb 2019)(Citation: Intego Shlayer Feb 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1564 Hide Artifacts\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1222.002 Linux and Mac Permissions\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1176.001 Browser Extensions\n- T1553.001 Gatekeeper Bypass\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1564.011 Ignore Process Interrupts\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1564.009 Resource Forking\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1548.004 Elevated Execution with Prompt\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0354": "# S0354 Denis\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0354\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0354\n\n[Denis](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0354) is a Windows backdoor and Trojan used by [APT32](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0050). [Denis](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0354) shares several similarities to the [SOUNDBITE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0157) backdoor and has been used in conjunction with the [Goopy](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0477) backdoor.(Citation: Cybereason Oceanlotus May 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1055.012 Process Hollowing\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1027.010 Command Obfuscation\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1560.002 Archive via Library\n- T1574 Hijack Execution Flow\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1139": "# S1139 INC Ransomware\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1139\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1139\n\n[INC Ransomware](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1139) is a ransomware strain that has been used by the [INC Ransom](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1032) group since at least 2023 against multiple industry sectors worldwide. [INC Ransomware](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1139) can employ partial encryption combined with multi-threading to speed encryption.(Citation: SentinelOne INC Ransomware)(Citation: Huntress INC Ransom Group August 2023)(Citation: Secureworks GOLD IONIC April 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1120 Peripheral Device Discovery\n- T1570 Lateral Tool Transfer\n- T1566 Phishing\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1652 Device Driver Discovery\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1491.001 Internal Defacement\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1134": "# S1134 DEADWOOD\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1134\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1134\n\n[DEADWOOD](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1134) is wiper malware written in C++ using Boost libraries. [DEADWOOD](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1134) was first observed in an unattributed wiping event in Saudi Arabia in 2019, and has since been incorporated into [Agrius](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1030) operations.(Citation: SentinelOne Agrius 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1561.001 Disk Content Wipe\n- T1027.009 Embedded Payloads\n- T1485 Data Destruction\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1531 Account Access Removal\n- T1561.002 Disk Structure Wipe\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0026": "# S0026 GLOOXMAIL\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0026\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0026\n\n[GLOOXMAIL](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0026) is malware used by [APT1](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0006) that mimics legitimate Jabber/XMPP traffic. (Citation: Mandiant APT1)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1071.005 Publish/Subscribe Protocols\n- T1102.002 Bidirectional Communication\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0281": "# S0281 Dok\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0281\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0281\n\n[Dok](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0281) is a Trojan application disguised as a .zip file that is able to collect user credentials and install a malicious proxy server to redirect a user's network traffic (i.e. [Adversary-in-the-Middle](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557)).(Citation: objsee mac malware 2017)(Citation: hexed osx.dok analysis 2019)(Citation: CheckPoint Dok)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1557 Adversary-in-the-Middle\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1543.001 Launch Agent\n- T1059.002 AppleScript\n- T1222.002 Linux and Mac Permissions\n- T1048.003 Exfiltration Over Unencrypted Non-C2 Protocol\n- T1553.004 Install Root Certificate\n- T1548.003 Sudo and Sudo Caching\n- T1056.002 GUI Input Capture\n- T1090.003 Multi-hop Proxy\n- T1547.015 Login Items\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1234": "# S1234 SplatCloak\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1234\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1234\n\n[SplatCloak](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1234) is a malware that disables EDR-related routines used by Windows Defender and Kaspersky to aid in evading detection.  [SplatCloak](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1234) has been deployed by [SplatDropper](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1232) and is known to be leveraged by [Mustang Panda](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0129) since 2025.(Citation: Zscaler PAKLOG CorkLog SplatCloak Splatdropper April 2025)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1036.001 Invalid Code Signature\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1106 Native API\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0579": "# S0579 Waterbear\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0579\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0579\n\n[Waterbear](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0579) is modular malware attributed to [BlackTech](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0098) that has been used primarily for lateral movement, decrypting, and triggering payloads and is capable of hiding network behaviors.(Citation: Trend Micro Waterbear December 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1055.003 Thread Execution Hijacking\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n-